I047,Indigenous Employment Initiative (IEI) Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Supporting the engagement of First Nations people in the aged care sector through the Indigenous Employment Initiative Program to subsidise employment and training to deliver non-clinical care and assist the capacity of the sector to deliver trauma informed, culturally safe care.",Resourcing: Up to $86 million over three years (2020-21 - 2022-23) for grants to subsidise the employment and training of First Nations people providing non-clinical aged care services to older First Nations people Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: The Department initiated a two stage program evaluation in 2022. Stage 1 resulted in the development of a Monitoring and Evaluation Framework (including Program Logic). A contract for Stage 2 was signed in November 2022. It commenced on 9 December 2022 and is expected to be finalised by August 2023.,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,,,,,,x,x,,,,
I048,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Languages Policy Partnership,Initiative,Partnership,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The First Nations Languages Policy Partnership (the Partnership) will drive coordinated action and progress against the Closing the Gap languages target (Target 16). The Partnership is a government commitment under the National Agreement on Closing the Gap (the National Agreement) and will identify opportunities to work more effectively across governments, reduce gaps and duplication and improve progress on Target 16.",Resourcing: $11 million for the language policy partnership and data infrastructure combined Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Arts Delivery Timeframe: Endorsed by Joint Council in December 2022 with the first meeting held in March and the second scheduled to be held in June 2023,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,x
I049,Formal Partnership Agreement,Initiative,Partnership,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A formal partnership agreement for shared decision making between Australian, state and territory governments and an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leadership Group (led by SNAICC - National Voice for our Children) will support genuine partnership in implementing Safe and Supported and its Action Plans. This is expected to be launched in mid 2023.",Resourcing: $2.87 million over five years for SNAICC to provide Secretariat Services to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leadership Group and policy advice to the Australian Government Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The Partnership Agreement will be for the length of the first Action Plans (2023 to 2026) then reviewed prior to the next set of Action Plans (2027 to 2031) and is expected to be finalised in early 2023.,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,x,,
I050,Justice Policy Partnership (JPP),Initiative,Partnership,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,x,x,,,"The Justice Policy Partnership brings together representatives from the Coalition of Peaks- external site, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander experts, and Australian, state and territory governments to take a joined-up approach to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander justice policy.  It is the first of 5 policy partnerships to be established under Priority Reform One of the National Agreement on Closing the Gap. Policy partnerships under the National Agreement will:  - drive Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community-led outcomes on Closing the Gap - enable Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representatives, communities and organisations to negotiate and implement agreements with governments to implement all priority reforms under the National Agreement and policy specific and place-based strategies to support Closing the Gap - support additional community-led development initiatives - bring together all government parties, together with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, organisations and communities to the collective task of Closing the Gap.","Resourcing: $7.6 million for the Justice Policy Partnership over three years (2021-24) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Continued engagement with the Justice Policy Partnership on policy priorities to inform the partnership's strategic direction, including quarterly meetings in March, June, September and December. Evaluation of the Justice Policy Partnership in 2023-24.",National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,x,,,
I051,Housing Policy Partnership (DSS),Initiative,Partnership,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In December 2022, Joint Council approved the establishment documents for the Housing Policy Partnership under Priority Reform One of the National Agreement on Closing the Gap. The Partnership will provide a forum for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people to have a genuine say in the design and delivery of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander housing services. The National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Housing Association (NATSIHA) will co-chair the Housing Policy Partnership with DSS and membership includes representatives from state and territory governments, the Coalition of Peaks and independent Indigenous representatives. The Partnership is expected to hold its first meeting in July 2023. ",Resourcing: $9.2 million over 3 years from 2022-23 to establish the Housing Policy Partnership. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness | Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: Joint Council endorsed the establishment of the Housing Policy Partnership in December 2022. First meeting of Housing Policy Partnership expected July 2023,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,x,
I053,Early Childhood Care and Development Policy Partnership (DOE),Initiative,Partnership,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This is one of five joined up partnerships will be established between the Commonwealth, states and territories and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representatives in priority policy areas justice, social and emotional wellbeing, housing, early childhood care and development, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages. The Early Childhood Care and Development Policy Partnership was established in August 2022.","Resourcing: $10.2 million over 3 years from 2022-23 for the establishment of the Early Childhood Care and Development Policy Partnership, including $4.9 million to SNAICC - National Voice for our Children as Co-Chair and co-secretariat Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: The Early Childhood Care and Development Policy Partnership was established in August 2022 with funding to 2025-26",National,N/A,,,,x,,,,x,,,,,,
I054,Working in Partnership (NATSIEC),Initiative,Reform,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Support formal establishment/ incorporation of the National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Education Council (NATSIEC). NATSIEC will provide a collective and representative voice on education matters relating to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.,Resourcing: $1.707 million in 2023 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2024,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I055,Embedding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander interests in the APS Reforms Charter of Partnerships and Engagement,Initiative,Reform,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Embedding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander interests in the APS Reforms Charter of Partnerships and Engagement,Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: To Be Confirmed,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I056,Partnership Stocktake - critical value assessment of Partnerships,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Undertake a 2023 stocktake of partnership arrangements The 2023 Partnership Stocktake will provide an opportunity to assess the existing and new partnerships against the baseline stocktake of 2022, to understand how and if arrangements have been strengthened to meet the strong partnership elements, key gaps in the partnership arrangements, and what further work is needed to support the Commonwealth to develop and strengthen partnerships.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I057,Gap Analysis to identify future Policy Partnerships,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Gap Analysis to identify future Policy Partnerships,Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Sectors to be decided in 2023 to 2024,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I058,Empowered Communities,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Empowered Communities leaders and the Commonwealth are working together in 10 regions, with further work underway to establish partnership arrangements with Empowered Communities leaders and the Commonwealth and state and territory governments across all ten sites.
I059,Six place-based partnerships,Initiative,Partnership,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The National Agreement commits all parties to jointly establish six place-based partnerships to focus on implementation of the National Agreement at the regional or local level. The selection of these locations will occur through jurisdictional shared decision-making processes including relevant states or territories, local government and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, as well as the Commonwealth through NIAA Regional Managers. The Commonwealth is supporting this engagement process by ensuring that the scope and implementation of place-based partnerships are in line with the strong partnership elements outlined in the National Agreement. This includes building on existing place-based approaches that align with community aspirations. Locations will be considered by Joint Council by November 2021 with partnerships to be in place by 2024.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 2024,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I060,Resources for family and children's services,Initiative,Resource,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Australian Institute of Family Studies and the Department of Social Services have funded Secretariat of National Aboriginal and Islander Child Care (National Voice for our Children), in the period 2020-2022, to develop tools and resources for Department of Social Services-funded family and children's services that promote and provide guidance on genuine partnerships between mainstream services and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Organisations. Resources are owned by Secretariat of National Aboriginal and Islander Child Care (National Voice for our Children) and published on the Secretariat of National Aboriginal and Islander Child Care (National Voice for our Children) and Australian Institute of Family Studies websites.","Resourcing: $300,000 over 2020-2022 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2023",National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,x,,
I061,"Closing the Gap Measure 3, Stronger ACCOs, Stronger Families Part 1 and Part 2",Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Stronger ACCOs, Stronger Families Part 1 - authored by SNAICC - National Voice for our Children - Identifies the strengths, opportunities, needs and barriers of Aboriginal community-controlled organisations (ACCOs) in delivering Commonwealth-funded child and family services.
I062,Home Interaction Program for Parents and Youngsters (HIPPY) - building the capacity of ACCOs,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Brotherhood of St Laurence (BSL) is currently participating in the Stronger ACCOs, Stronger Families Project (2021-2024). This project is aimed at increasing the involvement of ACCOs in the child and family sector. BSL's participation in this project will provide a baseline to a planned BSL project to enhance the current capacity of ACCO subcontractors, and looks to increase the number of these organisations subcontracted to deliver HIPPY either individually or through genuine partnerships.","Resourcing: Around $30 million per year from 1 July 2022 to 30 June 2027 for around 100 HIPPY sites across Australia. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The Stronger ACCOs, Stronger Families Project will finish on 30 June 2024. The grant agreement with BSL finishes on 30 June 2027.",National,N/A,,,,,x,,,x,,,,,,
I063,Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool - Disability Sector National Disability Footprint Phase 2 (Elements 3-7),Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"First Peoples Disability Network (FPDN) will build upon elements 1-2 of the Footprint from Phase 1. Implementation of these elements will enhance FPDN's capacity to represent the rights, priorities and interests of First Nations Peoples with disability through engagement, policy and sector and program development",Resourcing: $9.982 million (ex GST) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2024,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I064,Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool - Disability Sector National Disability Footprint Phase 1 (Elements 1-2),Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,First Peoples Disability Network (FPDN) will develop and implement a National Disability Footprint support network for the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander disability sector.,Resourcing: $1.426 million (ex GST) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2024,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I065,Building the community controlled sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool - Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services (ACCHS) Governance Training and Support Project,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"ACCHS Governance Training and Support Project provides funding to NACCHO to build capacity and capability, strengthen, and support governing bodies and leadership of ACCHS nationally.  This is funding under the Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool.",Resourcing: $1.963 million (ex GST) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2025,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I066,Building the community controlled sector joint virtual funding pool - National strategic roadmap for establishing an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander environmental health workforce,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"National Strategic Roadmap for an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Environmental Health Workforce Project provides funding to NACCHO to deliver a comprehensive co-designed National Strategic Roadmap to establish an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Environmental Health Workforce.
I067,Building the community controlled sector joint virtual funding pool - Indigenous Allied Health Australia - High school to deadly careers program,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Indigenous Allied Health Australia have been funded to deliver the High Schools to Deadly Careers Program that develops career aspirations, education and training pathways for young First Nations people including in the employment growth areas of health care and social assistance. The activity will focus on students in rural and remote communities across multiple jurisdictions.
I068,Building the community controlled sector joint virtual funding pool - Optimal Utilisation of the Medicare Benefits Schedule (MBS) Project,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool - Health Sector - Optimal Utilisation of the Medicare Benefits Schedule (MBS) Project provides funding to NACCHO to strengthen and support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community controlled health services (ACCHS) nationally. This activity will improve MBS claiming practices and the utilisation of other primary care payments to facilitate comprehensive multi-disciplinary health care to First Nations Australians. 
I069,Building the community controlled sector joint virtual funding pool - Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Community Controlled Health Registered Training Organisations (ACCHRTO) Project,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool - Health Sector - Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Community Controlled Health Registered Training Organisations (ACCHRTO) Project provides funding to NACCHO to determine workforce gaps and training needs of ACCHS nationally and identify actions and opportunities to build the capacity and capability of the ACCHRTO sector. 
I070,Development of an Agency Preferencing policy,Initiative,Reform,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Agency will continue to implement its Preferencing policy which includes a three tier preferencing model to support the allocation of IAS grant funding to majority owned and controlled First Nations organisations where the relevant IAS criteria have been met.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing - Data can be reported on as required,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I071,Support Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisations to deliver culturally safe and appropriate family dispute resolution,Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Provide funding to Aboriginal Community Controlled Organisations to assist Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families resolve post-separation parenting and property disputes through culturally safe and appropriate First Nations Family Dispute Resolution services.,Resourcing: A total of $8.3 million over three years from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Current funding ceases 30 June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I072,Sector Strengthening Plan evaluation methodology,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,x,,,,The evaluation approach will be applied to the Early Childhood Care and Development Sector Strengthening Plan as one of the mature community-controlled sectors. Findings from the evaluation will be used to inform development of additional plans.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I073,Jawun Partnership,Initiative,Program,Australian Public Service Commission,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Commonwealth is working with Jawun to increase the number of APS secondees to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander organisations from 110 to 130 annually in 2023 and increasing to 160 annually by 2027.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources (Secondments are funded on a user-pays basis (agencies are invoiced by Jawun)) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I074,Clause 55(b) 'meaningful proportion' guidance for agencies,Initiative,Other,Department of the Prime Minister & Cabinet,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Guidance advice for Clause 55(b) for the 2023-24 Budget was developed by the Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet and the Department of Finance, in collaboration with the Coalition of Peaks. This guidance was disseminated across Budget areas of public service agencies, by the Department of Finance.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Prime Minister | Minister for Finance Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I075,Business Case for Commonwealth Grants Connected Policy (Clause 55a),Initiative,Reform,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A business case for a Grant Connected Policy will be finalised for government consideration, to give effect to funding prioritisation policies under Clause 55(a) of the National Agreement.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 2024,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I076,Intermediary Support Services Pilot (THRYVE) (Early Childhood Care and Development Sector),Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Intermediary Support Services Pilot (THRYVE) - to significantly increase Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children's access to high quality, responsive, and culturally safe early learning services by piloting and starting to scale a capability building backbone entity across 3 jurisdictions.",Resourcing: $9.1 million (GST incl.) including co-contribution by DoE Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2030,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I077,Housing Peak Body Capacity Building,Initiative,Capacity Building,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To support and strengthen sector peaks and community controlled organisations Advise and guide Australian and Jurisdictional Governments on sector policy and budget matters and community-developed solutions.
I078,Agree additional sectors for Sector Strengthening Plans,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Working in partnership with all levels of government and the Coalition of Peaks to jointly agree additional sectors for Sector Strengthening Plans in 2023 as well as develop an evaluation methodology,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I081,Territories Stolen Generations Redress Scheme,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Territories Stolen Generations Redress Scheme,Resourcing: $378.6 million from 2021-2022 to 2025-2026 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,x,,,,
I082,Services Australia Customer Experience Survey,Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Service Delivery Excellence Division and the Indigenous and Remote Servicing branch are collaborating to investigate options to understand the level of racism experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander customers and how this may best be reported. The customer satisfaction and experience survey is not an appropriate mechanism for measuring racism experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Therefore, Services Australia is exploring a range of options that may help inform a baseline such as developing a targeted survey and using speech analytics to identify instances of racism in customer interactions. Consultation with community representatives will be important to confirm an appropriate way to approach this delicate subject. Discussions are being held across portfolios to ensure that an approach is developed that is consistent with other mechanism for measuring racism experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services Delivery Timeframe: Discussions are continuing with Department of Prime Minister and Cabinet to ensure any approach developed will be commensurate with other agencies they are coordinating with such as the ABS. Services Australia plan to develop a targeted survey to answer these questions in consultation with community representatives and the Department of Prime Minister and Cabinet.,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I083,Legislative amendments to the Public Service Act,Initiative,Reform,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In the second half of 2023, the Commonwealth will explore further opportunities to review the Public Service Act 1999 and related legislation to embed the Priority Reforms. Making these changes to the legislative frameworks that govern how the public service operates will better enable government organisations, and the services they provide, to be culturally safe and responsive to the needs of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians|Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I084,Jawun Alumni Program,Initiative,Program,Australian Public Service Commission,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Leveraging the learnings and experience of returning Jawun secondees (alumni) through coordination and integration of Jawun Alumni This enhances APS skills, knowledge and cultural capability and continue to build a cohort of APS members who can enable cultural change within and across APS agencies.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I085,Indigenous Graduate Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Defence,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,We recruit on behalf of a large number of Australian Government agencies who are seeking talent with a range of target disciplines.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Defence Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I086,First Nations-led research into transformation elements,Initiative,Research,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth, through the Partnership Working Group, and on behalf of all Parties to the National Agreement, will commission First Nations-led research to support a nationally consistent understanding of Priority Three transformational elements to better enable governments to deliver on their transformational commitments under the National Agreement.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I088,Developing the cultural competency and trauma responsiveness of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander and non-Indigenous child and family sector workforce engaged through the Department of Social Services (DSS) grant funding.,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The program will work nationally with up to 500 department-funded child and family sector service providers to undertake an assessment of their organisation's policies, practices, service models and staff capabilities with regard to their First Nations cultural awareness and trauma responsiveness. Training and development to address these needs will be delivered to child and family sector service providers until 30 June 2024.","Resourcing: $7.7 million over three years (2021-22 to 2023-24) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Grant opportunity was advertised in September 2022 and closed October 2022. The successful grant recipient was announced in February 2022. The first stage of the training program will be undertaken in the 2022-23 financial year. This includes assessments of cultural awareness and trauma responsive skills and capabilities within organisations and the delivery of training to multiple service providers. A key focus will be testing the training program within large service providers, with the goal of scaling the program rapidly if positive feedback is received and it can demonstrate it contributes to improved outcomes.",National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I089,Communications Material,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Development of guidance material to support Commonwealth Government agencies to deliver on this Implementation Plan and communicate about the Commonwealth's role in delivering on Closing the Gap, including a potential eLearning module and training",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I091,Commonwealth Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Workforce Strategy 2020-24,Commitment,Strategy,Australian Public Service Commission,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"An action plan will be developed to support the implementation of the second half of the Commonwealth Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Workforce Strategy 2020-24, recognising the Commonwealth's commitment to boost First Nations employment in the Public Service to 5% by 2030.","Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: An action plan will be developed in 2024 to support the implementation of the second half of the Commonwealth Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Workforce Strategy 2020-24, recognising the Commonwealth's commitment to boost First Nations employment in the Public Service to 5% by 2030 by end 2024",National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I093,"Identify, develop or strengthen an independent mechanism(s) to support, monitor, and report on the transformation of mainstream agencies and institutions",Initiative,Reform,Department of the Prime Minister & Cabinet|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Identify, develop or strengthen an independent mechanism, or mechanisms, that will support, monitor, and report on the transformation of mainstream agencies and institutions (Partnership Action)",Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians|Prime Minister Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I096,Indigenous Outreach Program,Initiative,Program,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Indigenous Outreach Program (IOP) is an established and specialist team working across ASIC to provide advice, insights and support to ensure ASIC's engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples is culturally appropriate and sensitive. The team also works with industry, service providers and other government agencies to influence system change and drive positive financial outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I097,Engagement by the Australian Human Rights Commission (AHRC),Initiative,Other,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Engagement by the Australian Human Rights Commission (AHRC) to protect human rights through the work of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Social Justice Commissioner and the Race Discrimination Commissioner.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I098,Additions to the Australian Public Service Survey of Trust,Initiative,Reform,Department of the Prime Minister & Cabinet,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Department of Prime Minister & Cabinet's Trust and Transparency Unit is scoping adding additional questions to the Survey of Trust in Australian public services to ask about First Nations people's experiences of racism and discrimination when using Australian public services. The Department is scoping the approach and questions through engagement with NIAA and others, including AIATSIS and the Coalition of the Peaks.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: Expected in 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I099,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Cultural Capability Framework,Commitment,Framework,Australian Public Service Commission,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Cultural Capability Framework,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Public Service Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing. Practical guidance to be launched through a communications campaign in first half 2023.,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I100,Mobile Infrastructure Report 2022,Initiative,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Annual report providing transparency and accountability over mobile network operator's investment in infrastructure, particularly in regional Australia. The report and associated data releases provide a useful evidence base on the state of mobile networks for policymakers, particularly when formulating policy responses to mobile coverage issues, including the digital inclusion of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in regional and remote Australia.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing with reports every 12 months,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,x,
I101,"Development of a national data collection of family, domestic and sexual violence statistics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",Initiative,Resource,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Development of a national data collection of family, domestic and sexual violence statistics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",Resourcing: 2021-22 Budget of $31.6 million has been redirected through the 2023-24 Budget to Indigenous-led  activities - details yet to be announced as the dedicated Action Plan is not finalised. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-23 to 2027-28,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,x,,
I102,Developing an Outcomes and Evidence Fund,Initiative,Funding,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Closing the Gap Outcomes and Evidence Fund (the Fund) provides funding to Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisations to co-design, trial and evaluate culturally appropriate projects that contribute to Outcomes 12 and 13, in urban, regional and/or remote areas across Australia. The Fund will focus on building upon the evidence base of what works for service delivery through co-designed reporting and evaluation, tailored data collection and analysis, optional outcomes-based funding, and program and project-specific evaluations.",Resourcing: $38.6 million from 2021-22 to 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2026-27,National,N/A,,,,x,x,,,,,,,x,,
I103,Consolidated national real-time reporting of deaths in custody,Initiative,Research,Attorney-General's Department | National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Explore options for enhanced reporting of First Nations deaths in custody; and establishing a qualitative research program that will incorporate the voices of First Nations people.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,x,,,
I104,First Nations Disability Data Scoping Study,Initiative,Research,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Access to relevant, appropriate, culturally safe, local and national First Nations disability data is critical to enabling community-controlled organisations and the wider disability sector, including government, to design and deliver responsive policies and programs to improve outcomes with and for First Nations peoples with disability. The First Nations Disability Data Scoping Study aims to identify data gaps in First Nations and disability prevalence, administrative and community-led data infrastructures. An important objective of the project is to deliver improvements in identifying the extent of disability in First Nations populations as previous siloed data collections have underestimated prevalence. The Scoping Study will develop a First Nations Data Strategy to strengthen approaches to collection, administration and analysis of First Nations and disability data to provide sufficient focus to all experiences of disability in regional, remote or urban contexts, gender, age or other intersectional lenses.
I105,Closing the Gap Community Data Portals  ,Initiative,Resource,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The National Agreement requires that, by 2023, the Parties will establish data projects in up to six locations across Australia. By mid 2023, nominations for all six locations had been received. These are South Australia (Western Suburbs of Adelaide); the Northern Territory (Maningrida); Western Australia (the Kimberley Region);  New South Wales (Blacktown Local Government Area); Queensland (Doomadgee) and Victoria (Gippsland). The Commonwealth, through NIAA, is playing a key coordinating role for the projects through the provision of secretariat functions. As part of its commitment to the Community Data Projects, the Commonwealth has also funded the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare to develop the infrastructure to support the secure portals and to assist with the development of secure data sharing protocols. The Community Data Projects will enable Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander organisations and communities to access and use locally relevant disaggregated data and information in partnership with all levels of government.
I106,Linking Crime and Justice Data,Initiative,Resource,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This funding will enable the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare (AIHW), in collaboration with ABS, to undertake a pilot to assess the feasibility of linking crime and justice data with other strategically important datasets.",Resourcing: $1.3 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2021 to 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I107,Fully established measurement frameworks and data development timeline for Socio-Economic Outcomes,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A priority action for the Commonwealth in the next 12 months is to ensure that all socio-economic outcomes have fully established measurement frameworks and data development timelines, and that data gaps, where identified, are given highest priority with regard to development actions. As outlined in the DDP, further work on supplementary data sources, and more frequent data collection, will also be prioritised.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I108,Fully established measurement frameworks and data development timeline for Priority Reforms,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A priority action for the Commonwealth in the next 12 months is to ensure that all Priority Reforms have fully established measurement frameworks and data development timelines, and that data gaps, where identified, are given highest priority with regard to development actions. As outlined in the DDP, further work on supplementary data sources, and more frequent data collection, will also be prioritised.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion by mid-2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I109,Connected Beginnings development of approach to data sharing in communities with overlap of programs,Initiative,Reform,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Connected Beginnings Program is committed to empowering communities with the confidence, power, and efficiency to know what's working and to solve complex social problems together. This is being achieved by ensuring communities have access to, and the capability to use, locally relevant data and information to set and monitor the implementation of efforts to embed Priority Reform Four, achieve their priorities and drive their own development. The program acknowledges Maiam nayri Wingara principles of Indigenous Data Sovereignty (IDS) and respects that the realisation of IDS will be different for each partnering community. To reduce the burden of data collection at the site level, the Commonwealth has undertaken data sharing arrangements across agencies and state and territory governments. The data collected is contextual and disaggregated at community level and incorporated into a set of interactive digital data dashboards that allows communities to track the numbers that are important to them and use this data to inform priorities and local decision-making. Data is relevant to the program goals around children thriving by the age of five and empowers sustainable self-determination and effective self-governance. The Department of Education is also working with colleagues across the Department of Social Services, the Department of Health and Aged Care, and the Department of Prime Minister and Cabinet to ensure a coordinated approach to data sharing in communities where there is an overlap of respective programs. This work will commence in early 2023 with bringing together communities to plan how this work can best achieve their data needs.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education|Minister for Health and Aged Care | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Commence early 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I110,Regional Data Hub,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Regional Data hub, as part of the Better Data Use to Support Delivery for Regional Australians program, will make location-specific data publicly accessible and searchable. ","Resourcing: $13.7 million over four years from 2020-21 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development and Local Government|Assistant Minister for Regional Development Delivery Timeframe: To be completed June 2024",National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I111,Progress in Australian Regions and Cities Dashboard,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Progress in Australian Regions and Cities Dashboard,"Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development and Local Government Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing Monthly release",National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I112,Data project - access to legal assistance,Initiative,Resource,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Attorney General's Department is working with the Australian Bureau of Statistics (ABS) to collect data on people accessing legal assistance services (Schedule D of the 2020-2025 National Legal Assistance Partnership Agreement (NLAP)). This will be a long-term project and will ultimately include collection of data on the number of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander clients accessing legal assistance and the type of service accessed. The aim is for the final reports to be aggregated and published on the ABS website.,Resourcing: A total of $2.2 million over five years from 2020-21 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Current funding ceases in 2024-25,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I114,Workforce Australia - Transition to Work,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Workforce Australia - Transition to Work is a demand driven program that provides intensive pre-employment assistance to disadvantaged young people aged 15-24 who are at a high risk of becoming long term unemployed.,Resourcing: $1.3 billion from 2023-24 to 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I115,Productivity Commission,Stakeholder,Commonwealth Government,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Funding for the Productivity Commission to deliver reporting under the National Agreement on Closing the Gap through assigned Productivity Commission activities. Deliverables include the data dashboard, annual data compilation reports and three yearly reviews.",Resourcing: $10.1 million over 4 years from 2020-21 (and $2.6 million per year ongoing) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians|Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
I116,First Nations Foreign Policy,Initiative,Reform,Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Government has committed to appointing an Ambassador for First Nations People, establishing an Office of First Nations Engagement within the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade; and implementing a First Nations approach to foreign policy. The appointment of an Ambassador for First Nations People; consultations with internal and external stakeholders on developing Australia's First Nations approach to foreign and trade policy. Establish an Office for First Nations Engagement to support the Ambassador.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Foreign Affairs Delivery Timeframe: The appointment of an Ambassador for First Nations People and establishment of an Office of First Nations Engagement will be completed within 2022-23.,National,N/A,,,,x,,x,,,,,,,,
I118,National Agent and Access Point (NAAP) program in Services Australia,Initiative,Program,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To enable regional and remote customers to access government services without needing to travel long distances to visit a service centre. At an Agent and an Access Point, customers can use self-service equipment including phones, free Wi-Fi and internet enabled self-service terminals with a scanning capability to upload documents. Additionally, Agents provide supported assistance for customers to register and access digital services to engage with government.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services|Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme Delivery Timeframe: The National Agent and Access Point locations are internally funded by Services Australia and contracts will continue in 2022-23 and will be ongoing.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I119,Improving Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander servicing in Services Australia,Initiative,Capacity Building,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Work continues to improve Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander servicing. Feedback from various Service Zones has been collated for analysis to help determine appropriate enhancements to our digital products and services. As an example, a successful collaboration between Language Services, Indigenous and Remote Services Branch and a Service Zone in Northern Australia resulted in new tools to help customers learn about myGov.  While digital has become a primary form of engagement with customers, Services Australia continues to support Australians who require assistance through other channels. We will ensure the needs of Indigenous peoples are met through the provision of tailored services which have been designed in collaboration with Indigenous customers and communities.  Any future strategies and initiatives, will also be co-designed with forums such as the National Indigenous Coalition. This will ensure the approach and outcomes are inclusive of the needs of First Nations people and consider accessibility (i.e. to devices, Wi-Fi, language barriers, etc.) that impact Indigenous and vulnerable customer cohorts.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services|Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing ,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I120,Universal Service Arrangements,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Legislated telecommunications obligation and associated funding, enabling people and businesses across Australia to access baseline fixed telephone and payphone services and access to both broadband and voice services regardless of location in Australia",Resourcing: $230 million per year for basic telephones; $40 million per year for payphones nationally Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing under law but the current funding agreement with Telstra ends 30 June 2032,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I121,Regional Telecommunications Review,Initiative,Other,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Every three years the Regional Telecommunications Independent Review Committee (RTIRC) examines the adequacy of regional telecommunications, consulting publicly including with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities.","Resourcing: Resourcing to be confirmed ahead of the next review Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Telecommunication Reviews take place every three years, the next review will be in 2024.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I122,Regional Tech Hub,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This service provides assistance and resources to regional, rural and remote consumers to help them maximise the benefits of available telecommunications products and services.","Resourcing: $916,000 in funding under the Regional Connectivity Program and a further $6 million has been committed under the Better Connectivity for Rural and Regional Australia Plan Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: 2024-25",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I123,Regional mobile infrastructure inquiry 2022-23,Initiative,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"On 31 March 2022, the Australian Government announced that it had directed the ACCC to conduct an inquiry into towers used in the supply of mobile telecommunications and other radio communications services in regional areas, and into the feasibility of providing mobile roaming during natural disasters or other emergencies. Outcomes from the report will benefit Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people living in regional and remote Australia.",Resourcing: $1.8 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I124,Regional Connectivity Program,Initiative,Program,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A competitive grants program targeting place-based telecommunications infrastructure that maximise economic and social opportunities in regional, rural and remote Australia.","Resourcing: $117.9 million (inc GST) under Round 1 was provided to support 130 'place-based' telecommunications infrastructure projects across regional, rural and remote Australia. An additional $137 million (inc GST) was provided under Round 2, including dedicated funding to improve telecommunications infrastructure through the Connecting Northern Australia initiative. In the October 2022 budget, the Government committed $200 million for a further two rounds of the RCP. Indicative funding of around $30 million of the Government's $200 million commitment towards further rounds will be set aside to improve telecommunications access in First Nations communities. Applications for  round 3 guidelines were opened on 20 March 2023 and close on 12 July 2023.This round includes $15m for funding of connectivity solutions for First Nations communities across regional Australia and $10m for First Nations communities in Central Australia. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Round 1 of RCP has been extended to include 23-24 FY, aligned to the timeframe for delivery of Round 2 and supported by a movement of funds to accommodate delays in project completion. Of a total of 223 funded projects under rounds 1 and 2, 38 projects are in First Nations communities providing $39.1 million (inc GST) of funding to projects in First Nation communities. NIAA should note that this figure is not based on a definition of a First Nation Community or a clearly defined mapping location or region, but the project description provided by the grantee. The RCP program is not a First Nations specific program and other RCP projects may also improve telecommunications outcomes for First Nations communities.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I125,NBN upgrades,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The government will invest $2.4 billion in upgrading an additional 1.5 million premises from Fibre to the Node (FTTN) to full fibre so that 90 per cent of fixed-line premises can receive services up to 1 gigabit per second. At least 660,000 of the premises upgraded will be in regional areas. The government has also provided a $480 million grant to NBN Co to upgrade NBN Fixed Wireless and Satellite services.",Resourcing: $2.4 billion over 4 years from 2022-23 for fixed-line upgrades. $480 million already provided in 2021-22 for fixed wireless upgrades. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Fixed-line upgrades to be complete by 2025. Fixed wireless upgrade due to be complete by the end of calendar year 2024.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I126,NBN Co's Regional Co-investment Fund,Initiative,Funding,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To improve broadband services over the NBN network to households, businesses and communities in regional areas - NBN Co has created this fund which it is using to invest alongside governments and local councils to improve broadband services where those investments would otherwise be sub-commercial.",Resourcing: $300 million from NBN Co Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I127,NBN Co Initiatives - connection,Initiative,Resource,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Connecting Aboriginal and Torres Strait Island services - with a focus on regional and remote Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander locations with low digital participation rates.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within NBN Co's existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I128,NBN Co Initiatives - capability,Initiative,Capacity Building,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"NBN Co evolved its digital capability tool with a version designed to better support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people released in July 2022. Digital literacy programs are ongoing including online resources to encourage safe and productive online participation and access to Government services online. NBN Co is supporting ongoing life skills (including digital skills) training through the JTLeadLikeAGirl program. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander business participation and innovation online is promoted through the Innovate with NBN Grants Program, particularly the indigenous category within the program.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within NBN Co's existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I129,Mobile Black Spot Program,Initiative,Program,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,A competitive grants program to improve mobile phone infrastructure in regional and remote Australia.,"Resourcing: $311.4 million in total program funding nationally, including $40 million allocated to the Improving Mobile Coverage Round. As at 18 May 2023, 1079 Rounds 1-5 base stations complete (90%). The rollout of Round 5A has commenced in 2022-23. The Government committed $400m in additional funding in the October 2022-23 Budget for further mobile investment on highways and blackspots, a part of the Better Connectivity Plan for Regional and Rural Australia.  A $50m Round 7 of the MBSP opened for applications on 20 March and will close on 12 July 2023, with $7.5m dedicated to solutions in First Nations communities across regional Australia. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: 2024-25, with the bulk of Rounds 1-5A base stations expected to be completed by 30 June 2023.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I130,First Nations Digital Inclusion Advisory Group,Network,Advisory Body,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,To support the community and government to Close the Gap in digital inclusion.,Resourcing: Funding of $2.5 million over five years to support the establishment of the First Nations Digital Inclusion Advisory Group Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Funding provided up until 2026-27.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I131,Connecting Northern Australia initiative,Initiative,Funding,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,A competitive grants program targeting place-based telecommunications infrastructure that maximise economic and social opportunities in northern Australia,"Resourcing: As part of the 2021-22 Budget, the previous Government committed $66.5 million (ex GST) in dedicated funding to improve telecommunications infrastructure in Northern Australia. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Communications Delivery Timeframe: Funding is allocated over 2021-22 and 2022-23 and 2023-24 for delivery through RCP Round 2.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I133,Tax Help,Initiative,Program,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The ATO offers Tax Help services through community centres, with nearly 30 centres specifically supporting Indigenous Australians. We have a dedicated Indigenous helpline, and we have translated Tax Help promotional posters into 13 Indigenous languages. We also offer free education sessions on tax and superannuation to schools with high Indigenous populations.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Financial Services|Assistant Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,x
I134,United Nations (UN) International Decade of Indigenous Languages 2022-32,Commitment,Other,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Australian Government's participation in the International Decade embeds a best-practice approach to working in partnership with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people to improve outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Arts Delivery Timeframe: 2022-32,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I135,Northern Territory Government - National Partnership on Northern Territory Remote Aboriginal Investment - Remote Australia Strategy Implementation Plan - Aboriginal Interpreter Service,Commitment,Action Plan,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This project works to improve the quality of, and access to, Aboriginal language interpreting services through a professional interpreter workforce and a sustainable interpreting service delivery model.",Resourcing: $32.818 million from 2015-16 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2024,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I136,National Indigenous Languages Survey,Initiative,Other,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The National Indigenous Languages Survey is currently the only mechanism through which governments can measure progress against Target 16.,Resourcing: $1.2 million over 2 years from 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Arts Delivery Timeframe: Completed in June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I137,Northern Territory Government Aboriginal Interpreter Service - Legal Interpreting Services,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,This project aims to assist in alleviating the language barrier faced by Aboriginal people in the Northern Territory by providing Aboriginal interpreting services through 8 legal services and law and justice agencies.,Resourcing: $8.721 million from 2015-16 to 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I138,Northern Territory Government Aboriginal Interpreter Service - Cross Border Interpreting Services,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This project aims to alleviate language barriers faced by Aboriginal people and service providers in Anangu Pitjantjatjara Yankunytjatjara (APY) and Ngaanyatjarra Lands by improving access to and utilisation of Aboriginal interpreters and language services by government, service provider and Aboriginal people in APY and Ngaanyatjarra Lands.",Resourcing: $3.95 million from 2013-14 to 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I139,National Accreditation Authority for Translators and Interpreters (NAATI) - Indigenous Language Interpreter Certification,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The project supports the expression, engagement and conservation of First Nations culture through increasing the number of certified First Nations language interpreters in Australia. NAATI provides a minimum of 20 First Nations language certification testing opportunities and provide a minimum of 900 hours of employment to First Nations people annually in the delivery of the project.",Resourcing: $3.549 million from 2014-15 to 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I140,Indigenous Languages and Arts (ILA) program,Initiative,Program,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Indigenous Languages and Arts (ILA) program,Resourcing: Approx. $30 million annual ongoing investment that is inclusive of $23.4 million of additional Closing the Gap investment over four years from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Arts Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing grant program with an annual open and targeted competitive grant opportunity,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I141,Aboriginal Interpreters Western Australia (AIWA) Aboriginal Corporation - Kimberley Aboriginal Language Interpreting,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,This project supports the delivery of Kimberley Aboriginal Language Interpreting by the Aboriginal Interpreting Western Australia and aims to increase the accessibility and availability of Kimberley Indigenous language interpreting for service providers and government agencies engaging with individuals and families.,Resourcing: $4.0 million from 2014-15 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,State,Western Australia,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I142,Native Title Legislation Amendment Act 2021 (NTLAA),Initiative,Reform,Attorney-General's Department | National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"All measures commenced as of September 2021, and include changes that provide more flexibility to native title claim groups to set their internal processes, and to streamline native title claims resolutions and decision-making.",Resourcing: $7.1 million from 2021-22 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I143,Native title compensation work with states and territories,Initiative,Other,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,x,,,The Commonwealth continues to regularly meet with the National Native Title Council and state and territory officials with responsibility for native title to discuss emerging issues about native title compensation.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I144,Native Title Anthropologists Grants Program,Initiative,Program,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Native Title Anthropologists Grants Program supports the work of anthropologists in the native title system to resolve native title claims,Resourcing: $1.069 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Current grant round will cease in June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I145,National Native Title Tribunal and the Federal Court of Australia's native title practice area,Initiative,Program,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,National Native Title Tribunal and the Federal Court of Australia's native title practice area for the resolution of ongoing native title claims,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I145,Yeddung Mura External Reporting Site (the External Reporting Site) ,Initiative,Reform,ACT Corrective Services (ACTCS),Territory Government,x,,,,,"The Yeddung Mura External Reporting Site (the External Reporting Site) was established in July 2021 to help facilitate culturally appropriate supervision of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander offenders who are subject to community-based corrections orders, such as Parole Orders, Intensive Correction Orders and Good Behaviour Orders. The External Reporting Site aims to promote successful completion of orders, foster linkages with other services based at the site, and provide an alternative model of trauma-informed care for clients with complex mental health issues. It also helps to increase cultural awareness and culturally appropriate practice with our Community Corrections staff. The External Reporting Site aims to contribute to the government priorities of reducing re-offending and providing safe, secure, decent, and humane management of offenders. It aims to achieve this by: • Providing culturally sensitive and holistic justice and social support services, • Strengthening relationships between ACTCS and First Nations clients, and • Reducing the risk of reoffending and behaviour that can lead to over-representation and recidivism in First Nations communities.",n.a.,"Territory, Regional",ACT,Fadden,,,,,x,,,,x,x,x,,x
I146,Federal Court of Australia to engage with external Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander mediators,Initiative,Reform,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Federal Court of Australia to engage with external Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander mediators to appropriately co-mediate disputes.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I146,NaraGanaWali Program,Initiative,Program,Yerrabi Yurwang Child and Family Corporation,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,,,,,,"The Yerrabi Yurwang Child and Family Corporation's NaraGanaWali Program provides in-home, or wherever, cultural and clinical interventions, counselling, life-skills education and referral pathways for Aboriginal Women and Children at risk of, or experiencing Family, Domestic and Sexual Violence (FDSV).",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,,x,,,x,x,x,x,x,,
I147,Federal Court of Australia appointment of an Indigenous Registrar,Initiative,Resource,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Federal Court of Australia appointment of an Indigenous Registrar to co-mediate Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander disputes.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I147,Safe and Connected Youth Project (Youth Coalition of the ACT),Initiative,Funding,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,,,,,,"From 2020 onwards, the ACT Government provided additional funding helped to continue and expand the capacity of the Safe and Connected Youth project, to respond to increased demand for an integrated service for addressing youth at risk of or experiencing homelessness and to address the complexity of cases resulting from the COVID-19 pandemic.",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,,,,,,,x,,x,x,
I148,10 new Indigenous Protected Areas,Initiative,Target,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,x,,,The Commonwealth is providing new funding to support 10 new Indigenous Protected Areas - adding around 4.8 million hectares to the National Reserve System and contributing to the Government's commitment to protect and conserve 30 per cent of our landmass by 2030.,Resourcing: $231.5 million to extend program to June 2028 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Environment and Water|Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2028,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I149,Double the number of Indigenous Rangers,Initiative,Target,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Government will double the number of Indigenous rangers to 3,800 by the end of the decade (with a focus on gender equality) and expand the Indigenous Protected Areas Programs.",Resourcing: $636 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Delivery by 2030,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
I150,National Strategic Framework for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples' Mental Health and Social and Emotional Wellbeing,Commitment,Strategy,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Refresh of the Framework and an Implementation Plan are high priorities for the Social and Emotional Wellbeing Policy Partnership (SEWBPP) who held their first meeting in March 2023. NIAA will support the Refresh and Implementation Plan, once preferred approach is determined by the SEWBPP.",Resourcing: Funding for this will be determined following receipt of advice to Government and agreement to what is proposed. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: To be confirmed based on advice from SEWBPP,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I151,Indigenous Advancement Strategy - 1.3 Safety and Wellbeing Program - Social and Emotional Wellbeing (SEWB) and Mental Health and Suicide Prevention activities,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Safety and Wellbeing Program is about making communities safer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and enabling them to enjoy similar levels of physical, emotional and social wellbeing as those enjoyed by non-Indigenous people. 
I152,Stage Three Wiyi Yani U Thangani (Women's Voices) project,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Support the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Social Justice Commissioner, Ms June Oscar AO, to deliver the Wiyi Yani U Thangani (Women's Voices) Project. Commissioner Oscar will convene a First Nations Women and Girls National Summit, supported by the National Indigenous Australians Agency, in May 2023. Other deliverables include a research project to identify strengths based approaches for First Nations women and girls, a website for First Nations Gender Justice and Equality, and a National Framework for Action to be launched March 2024.",Resourcing: $2.8 million over three years (2021-22 to 2023-24) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion by June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,x,,
I153,Specialised Family Violence Services - Aboriginal Community Controlled Family Violence Prevention Legal Service (FVPLS) providers,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Specialised Family Violence Services - Aboriginal Community Controlled Family Violence Prevention Legal Service (FVPLS) providers,Resourcing: $68.6 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24 to 2024-25,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I154,National Principles to Address Coercive Control,Commitment,Framework,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To create a shared national understanding of coercive control to help raise awareness of the issue, inform more effective responses to family and domestic violence, and promote more consistent support and safety outcomes for victim-survivors, particularly for First Nations women who are often misidentified as perpetrators of violence.","Resourcing: Part of a $4.7 million measure from 2021-22 to 2022-23 to strengthen criminal justice responses to sexual assault, sexual harassment and coercive control. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Expected to be finalised in 2023",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I155,Funding the National Family Violence Prevention Legal Services Forum,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Forum works in collaboration with Family Violence Prevention Legal Services across the country to increase access to justice for First Nations people experiencing or at risk of family violence, especially women and children.",Resourcing: $3 million (2022-23 to 2024-25) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2025-26 (Yet to be confirmed),National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I156,Funding for the Family Violence Prevention Legal Services (FVPLS),Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Sixteen FVPLS providers are funded under the Indigenous Advancement Strategy to provide culturally safe legal services, non-legal services and wrap around support to First Nations victims and survivors of family violence and sexual assault; including increased access to legal assistance, specialist services, trauma aware and healing-informed support. In the six months from 1 July 2021 to 30 December 2021, FVPLS providers supported over 5,000 clients with legal and non-legal services across 195 locations.",Resourcing: $92 million (2020-21 to 2022-23) in direct funding to the FVPLS providers to deliver on-the-ground services. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I157,Better support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women and children who have experienced or are experiencing family violence,Initiative,Capacity Building,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Better support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women and children who have experienced or are experiencing family violence, by building the capability and quality of family safety services for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander clients.",Resourcing: $6 million to build the capability and quality of family safety services for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander clients Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I158,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Family Safety Services,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"18 providers are funded via the Indigenous Advancement Strategy (IAS) to deliver 20 activities under the 3rd and 4th Action Plans (3AP and 4AP) of the National Plan to Reduce Violence Against Women and their Children (2010-2022). Services funded under the 3AP and 4AP invest in critical activities that support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander family safety.
I159,National Plan to End Violence Against Women and Children 2022-23 (National Plan),Commitment,Action Plan,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Target 13 of the Closing the Gap (CtG) agreement seeks to ensure families and households are safe by reducing the rate of all forms of family violence and abuse against Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women and children. The National Plan is the Commonwealth and state and territory government's commitment to a country free of gender-based violence - where all people live free from fear and violence and are safe at home, at work, at school, in the community and online. The CtG agreement is embedded with in the National Plan, including an acknowledgement of the disproportionate rates of violence against Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women and children women.","Resourcing: To be confirmed: Clear actions to implement the National Plan will be outlined in two supporting five-year Action Plans and a dedicated Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Action Plan. These Action Plans will detail the investment, efforts and actions the Commonwealth, state and territory governments will take to implement the National Plan. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2032",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I162,National Escaping Violence Payment Trial,Initiative,Funding,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Escaping Violence Payment is a two year national trial supporting eligible victim-survivors, from all backgrounds including First Nations, experiencing intimate partner violence to establish a home free from violence.",Resourcing: $216.6 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2022-23,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I163,Escaping Violence Payment place-based trial,Initiative,Funding,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The trial will be delivered by an Indigenous-led organisation located in the Cairns region. It aims to provide specialised and culturally-appropriate support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander victim-survivors of intimate partner violence to establish a home free from violence.,Resourcing: $7 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-23 to 2023-24,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I164,Enhance women and children's safety,Initiative,Reform,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Enhance women and children's safety by supporting a nationally coordinated approach to education and training for family, domestic and sexual violence frontline workers, family law practitioners, the judiciary and the justice sector more broadly. Continue funding to maintain the National Domestic and Family Violence Bench Book and delivery of Family Violence in the Court Training (FViCT) Program.","Resourcing: A total of $380,000 over five years from 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Current funding for Bench Book and FViCT funding ceases 30 June 2026",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I165,Domestic Violence Units (DVUs) and Health Justice Partnerships (HJPs),Initiative,Funding,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Increase funding for DVUs and HJPs, including dedicated funding for outreach in rural, remote and regional areas in all jurisdictions other than the ACT.",Resourcing: A total of $17.1 million over four years from 2021 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: The measure commenced on 1 July 2021 and is ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I166,"Development of resources that aim to prevent and respond to violence against women and girls with a disability, and improve service responses when violence occurs.",Initiative,Resource,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Frequently violence in First Nations communities happens alongside multiple, intersecting and layered forms of discrimination and disadvantage affecting safety, one of which maybe disability. The review and redevelopment of products and supports will ensure they are accessible for all Australians, including First Nations people.",Resourcing: $9.3 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2023-24,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I167,Additional funding for Children's Contact Services,Initiative,Funding,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Reduce safety risks to family law system users, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families, by increasing the availability of Children's Contact Services, including in regional and remote locations.",Resourcing: A total of $101.4 million over four years from 2021 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I168,"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Advisory Council on family, domestic and sexual violence (Advisory Council)",Network,Advisory Body,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Advisory Council played a key advisory role in the development of the National Plan. The Advisory Council are leading the drafting of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Action Plan which will expressly seek to address Target 13.,Resourcing: $1 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2023-24,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I169,National Strategy to Prevent and Respond to Child Sexual Abuse Supporting Healing for Families (NIAA),Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This initiative will co-design, deliver, and evaluate Indigenous-led, place-based, and trauma-aware healing initiatives for Indigenous victims and survivors of child sexual abuse and their families, across five locations in Australia, as part of the first phase of Commonwealth funding under the National Strategy to Prevent and Respond to Child Sexual Abuse 2021-2030.",Resourcing: $10.9 million Starting in 2021 until December 2025 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 1/12/2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,x,,
I170,National Strategy to Prevent and Respond to Child Sexual Abuse Health Services Capability Building.,Initiative,Capacity Building,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This activity, funded under the first National Action Plan of the National Strategy to Prevent and Respond to Child Sexual Abuse 2021-2030, will design, trial and evaluate resources for health workers to improve first disclosure conversations and referrals to specialist services. The activity aims to improve the responsiveness of the primary health care system in order to drive improved support and referral outcomes for First Nations victims and survivors of child sexual abuse.",Resourcing: $3.8 million from 2021 to June 2025 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,x,,
I172,Dedicated Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Action Plan,Commitment,Action Plan,Department of Social Services|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Dedicated Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Action Plan. The release of the dedicated Action Plan will detail investment, efforts and actions of the Commonwealth, state and territory governments.","Resourcing: $145.3 million over 4 years from 2023–24,  to support activities which address immediate safety concerns for First Nations women and children who are experiencing, or at risk of experiencing, family, and domestic and sexual violence.  Plus $7.7 million over 3 years from 2023-24 to establish a First Nations Peak Body, aligning to priority actions in the dedicated action plan. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians|Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24 to 2027-28. To commence from July 2023",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I173,Expanding culturally responsive support for First Nations Australians in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia,Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Funding has been provided to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to employ an additional 11 Indigenous Liaison Officers, dedicated Judicial Registrar and family consultant which will provide support to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people engaging with the family law system and support the expansion of Specialist Indigenous Lists to all Court registries.",Resourcing: $8.7 million over 4 years (from 2022-23) in addition to $1.7 million in ongoing funding from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Expansion to be complete by June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I174,Children and Family Intensive Support (CaFIS),Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"During 2021-22, the redesigned CaFIS grants were finalised. Eleven of the 12 successful applicants were ACCOs and a 12th ACCO is working in consortium with a non-Indigenous organisation. During 2022-23, the CaFIS program will continue to embed within the Aboriginal communities in which it is delivered.",Resourcing: $45 million from 2021-22 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: CaFIS grants are for 5 years up until the 30 June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I176,Improving multidisciplinary responses (IMR) to First Nations families with multiple and complex needs,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Redesigning service models to effectively respond to multiple and interrelated issues in families (such as family violence, mental and physical health problems, substance use and disability), which may lead to child abuse and neglect. This includes embedding the five elements of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait islander Child Placement Principle in the service model re-design",Resourcing: $49 million over five years (2021-22 to 2025-26) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe:  Grant opportunity closed 5 April 2023 and activities will commence in July 2023. Completion due by June 2027 ,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,x,,
I177,Standing Council of Attorneys-General consideration of the minimum age of criminal responsibility,Initiative,Reform,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In partnership with states and territories, a proposal to be developed to increase the minimum age of criminal responsibility, paying particular attention to eliminating the overrepresentation of First Nations' children in the criminal justice system. ",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I178,Reduce the risk of First Nations young people coming in child protection and justice systems,Initiative,Target,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Youth related activities that reduce the risk of First Nations young people coming into contact with the child protection and justice systems that support wellbeing and positive behaviour including diversionary activities for at risk youth to provide intensive case management and wrap-around supports and referrals.,Resourcing: $308 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I179,Support ATSILS to provide legal assistance to families in coronial inquiries and during the process,Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A) Support ATSILS to provide legal assistance to families of deceased individuals in coronial inquiries, and to support clients involved in complex and/or expensive criminal cases (2021-22 to 2024-25)
I180,"Optional Protocol to the Convention against Torture and Other Cruel, Inhuman or Degrading Treatment and Punishment (OPCAT)",Initiative,Funding,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Funding to jurisdictions to support their implementation of the OPCAT which mandates regular inspections of places of detention, including prisons and youth detention centres in all states and territories, and in facilities under the control of the Commonwealth. Indigenous Australians are overrepresented in detention populations. This initiative will aid in improving rehabilitation outcomes for detainees.",Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: To Be Confirmed,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I181,Development of a National Bench Book,Initiative,Resource,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Development of a National Bench Book on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples and the Legal System. The Australasian Institute of Judicial Administration is funded for the development and online publication of the National Bench Book on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples and the Legal System as a practical and culturally-sensitive resource for judicial officers and legal professionals providing justice and legal assistance services to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in the criminal and civil justice systems.,"Resourcing: $642,000 over three years from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Publication of the Bench Book by the end of 2024. Completion of an evaluation by 30 June 2025.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I182,"Nationally coordinated approach to education and training for family, domestic and sexual violence",Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Enhance women and children's safety by supporting a nationally coordinated approach to education and training for family, domestic and sexual violence frontline workers, family law practitioners, the judiciary and the justice sector more broadly. Provide funding to develop and deliver a new training package for legal practitioners on coercive control and family, domestic, and sexual violence training package for law enforcement.","Resourcing: A total of $900,000 over four years from 2022-23 for coercive control training for legal practitioners. A total of $4.1 million over four years from 2022-23 to develop & deliver a training package for law enforcement. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Current funding for both measures ceases 30 June 2025",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I183,Justice Reinvestment (NIAA),Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Enable up to 30 communities to establish locally tailored initiatives that address the underlying causes of First Nations peoples contact with the criminal justice system. Establish an independent national justice reinvestment unit that will assist communities to develop and evaluate justice reinvestments, ensuring valuable lessons can be learned and shared across the country",Resourcing: $69 million from 2022-23 to 2024-25 and $12.5 million from 2022-23 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General|Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I184,Establish justice reinvestment initiatives (AGD),Initiative,Program,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Establish justice reinvestment initiatives across the country with a focus on turning the tide on incarceration and deaths in custody. A National Justice Reinvestment program will be established, to support up to 30  place-based community-led justice reinvestment initiatives and an independent National Justice Reinvestment Unit to coordinate and support justice reinvestment initiatives at a national level. In 2023, in partnership with First Nations communities and relevant justice reinvestment experts, the Commonwealth will design the justice reinvestment program and support establishment of an independent National Justice Reinvestment Unit.",Resourcing: $81.5 million from 2022-23 to 2026-27 then $20 million ongoing from 2026-27. An additional $10 million over four years from 2023-24 has also been allocated for justice reinvestment in Central Australia. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing measure with the first phase of delivery completed by June 2027,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I185,Build National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Island Legal Services (NATSILS) capacity,Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department | National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,This funding will support NATSILS' important contribution to law reform and policy development to address inequalities in the legal system.,Resourcing: $1 million from 2022-23 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I186,Indigenous Advancement Strategy Safety and Wellbeing Program,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Funding provided to complement efforts by states and territories to improve justice outcomes.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I187,Increase Aboriginal and Torres Strait Is-lander Legal Services (ATSILS) capacity to ensure First Nations people's access to legal assistance,Initiative,Capacity Building,Attorney-General's Department,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A) Funding provided to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Legal Service (ATSILS) Legal assistance through the National Legal Assistance Partnership Agreement 2020-25.  B) One-off additional funding for ATSILS for the 2023-24 financial year, through AGD's grant agreement with the peak body, National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Legal Services (NATSILS).",Resourcing: A) More than $440 million over five years  B) $21 million in 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Attorney-General Delivery Timeframe: 2020-25,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,x,,,
I188,Regional First Home Buyer Scheme,Initiative,Funding,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Regional First Home Buyer Support Scheme will help 10,000 first home buyers a year in regional Australia to buy a home. The Scheme will provide a government guarantee of up to 15 per cent of the purchase price of a home for eligible first home buyers. This will enable eligible applicants with a 5 per cent deposit to avoid paying Lenders Mortgage Insurance.","Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: From 1 October 2022, 10,000 Regional First Home Buyer Guarantee places were made available to eligible regional first home buyers. 10,000 guarantees are available annually, but likely to cease each year once 10,000 places have been allocated.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I190,National Water Grid Fund,Initiative,Funding,"Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is committing to fund water infrastructure projects for regional and remote First Nations communities. This funding will help improve essential water services and water quality, which are often below Australian standards. The Commonwealth will work closely with stakeholders and state and territory governments to identify and deliver eligible projects.",Resourcing: $150 million over four years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Environment and Water Delivery Timeframe: 2023 to 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I191,National Housing Finance and Investment Corporation (NHFIC),Stakeholder,Other,Department of Social Services|Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Through the Affordable Housing Bond Aggregator (AHBA), NHFIC provides low cost, long-term loans to registered community housing providers to support the provision of more social and affordable housing.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I192,Housing Supply and Affordability Council,Stakeholder,Commonwealth Government,Department of Social Services|Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth has committed to establishing a National Housing Supply and Affordability Council (the Council). The Council will be advised by experts from a diverse range of relevant fields including finance, economics, urban development, residential construction, urban planning and social housing sectors and will increase housing supply and improve housing affordability. Legislation to establish the Council lists a fields that a person must have expertise in which includes the housing needs of Aboriginal persons and Torres Strait Islanders.","Resourcing: $15.2 million over 4 years from 2022-23 (and $4.4 million per year ongoing) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: An interim Council was established on 1 January 2023, The Government is introducing legislation to make the Council a statutory body in the next sitting of Parliament with the intention for it to commence 1 July 2023.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I193,Housing Australia Future Fund,Initiative,Funding,Department of Social Services|National Indigenous Australians Agency|Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth will establish a $10 billion Housing Australia Future Fund which will build 30,000 new social and affordable housing properties in the first 5 years. Once established and the fund generates returns, investment returns will be used to fund social and affordable housing projects.","Resourcing: A portion of the investment returns will be used to fund acute housing needs on an ongoing basis. This includes $200 million for the repair, maintenance and improvements of housing in remote Indigenous communities. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: Expected to be legislated in  2023-24",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I194,Housing Accord,Network,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,x,x,,,"The Housing Accord brings together all levels of government, investors, and the residential development, building and construction sector to unlock quality, affordable housing supply over the medium term. The Accord lays the groundwork to improving affordability by addressing Australia's housing supply challenges and enabling the delivery of more social and affordable housing.","Resourcing: $350 million over 5 years from 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: Housing Accord delivery will be from 2024 to 2029 of the initial agreement, with work commencing immediately to ensure delivery of new housing supply.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I195,Help to Buy,Initiative,Funding,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Help to Buy is a shared equity scheme allowing 10,000 eligible home buyers to purchase a new or existing home with an equity contribution from the Commonwealth Government. The Commonwealth has committed to providing an equity contribution of up to a maximum of 40 per cent of the purchase price of a new home and up to a maximum of 30 per cent of the purchase price for an existing home.",Resourcing: $324.6 million over 4 years from 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: To be confirmed,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I196,Northern Territory Housing and Essential Infrastructure,Initiative,Resource,Department of Social Services|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Commonwealth has committed to providing resourcing to the Northern Territory Government to progress urgent work on housing and essential infrastructure on Northern Territory homelands.,Resourcing: $25 million 2022-23 and $75 million 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: 2022 to 2024,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I197,New Remote Housing Agreement with the Northern Territory,Commitment,Agreement,Department of Social Services|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Commonwealth has committed to negotiating a new remote housing agreement with the Northern Territory Government when the current one expires on 30 June 2023.,Resourcing: $111.7 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 1 July 2023 to 30 June 2024,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I198,National Housing and Homelessness Agreement (NHHA),Commitment,Agreement,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The NHHA is currently the primary vehicle for the provision of Commonwealth funding to help support the states in the delivery of housing and homelessness services and programs. The NHHA aims to improve access to secure and affordable housing for all Australians across the housing spectrum. As current funding arrangements under the NHHA are due to expire on 30 June 2023, a one-year extension of around $1.7 billion to the NHHA has been offered to 30 June 2024, including an additional $67.5 million of funding to assist the sector in tackling the homelessness challenges revealed in the 2021 Census. This extension will allow further time to work with states and key stakeholders to implement the Government's housing agenda.  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are a priority homelessness cohort under the current NHHA. Each state and territory is required to report how much funding is attributed to each priority homelessness cohort each year, where the disaggregation of data is possible.",Resourcing: $1.6 billion per year to states and territories. Funding under the NHHA for each state and territory in 2022-23 - NSW $497.8 million - Vic $418.7 million - QLD $342.2 million - WA $182.2 million - SA $116.9 million - Tas $37.4 million - ACT $29.4 million - NT $21.4 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing|Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: State and territory bilateral agreements expire 30 June 2023. The Government has offered states and territories a year of additional funding to 30 June 2024.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I199,Indigenous Home Ownership Program,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Delivered through Indigenous Business Australia (IBA). The program provides low deposit, low priced home loans for Indigenous Australians who face barriers to accessing mainstream finance. The IBA Regional Construction Measure was extended to 2024-25. IBA has assisted over 20,600 Indigenous families into home ownership over the last 40 years. IBA has issued an average of over 500 home loans per year since 2011-12 under IHOP.","Resourcing: 2020-21 Budget, IBA received an additional investment of $150 million over 3 years (2020-21 to 2023-24) for the Regional Construction Measure to deliver 360 new housing construction loans in regional Australia. In the 2022-23 Budget the delivery time for IBA to provide RCMS loans was extended to 2024-25. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I200,Improving Housing in Northern Territory homelands,Initiative,Target,Department of Social Services|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Improving Housing in Northern Territory homelands for housing and essential infrastructure on Northern Territory homelands. Including improvements to water, power and community facilities, as well as housing upgrades, extensions and new builds.","Resourcing: $25 million 2022-23
I201,Commonwealth Rent Assistance (CRA),Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Provide financial support for the cost of renting for low income households.,Resourcing: Estimated $5.5 billion in 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing social security payment,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I202,Barkly Regional Deal,Initiative,Agreement,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Commitment between the Australian Government, the Northern Territory Government and Barkly Regional Council to improve the productivity and liveability of the Barkly region by stimulating economic growth, improving social outcomes and supporting local Aboriginal leadership.","Resourcing: The Barkly Regional Deal is an $84.7 million deal between the Barkly regional Council ($3 million), Northern Territory Government ($30 million) and the Australian Government ($51.7 million). Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Assistant Minister for Regional Development Delivery Timeframe: 2029-30",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,x,
I204,ATO Reach Out Small Business Support Program,Initiative,Program,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The ATO's Reach Out program helps Indigenous small businesses meet their tax, super and registry obligations by providing tailored education and support. From 1 July to 31 December 2022, we participated in two face-to-face events with Yarpa Indigenous Business and Employment Hub, which included delivering 40 sessions to approx. 780 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander participants.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Homelessness|Minister for Housing|Minister for Small Business Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I205,Protecting First Nations Intellectual Property,Initiative,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Drawing from the findings of a Productivity Commission inquiry into the market for First Nations arts and crafts, the Commonwealth will work with First Nations to establish standalone legislation to protect First Nations peoples traditional knowledge and cultural expressions, as well as reviewing the operation of the Indigenous Art Code.",Resourcing: To be confirmed Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: To be confirmed,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I206,ATO compliance with Commonwealth Indigenous Procurement Policy,Initiative,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The ATO continues to be a significant contributor to the Treasury Portfolio's performance against the government's 3% targets for procurement from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander businesses.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Financial Services|Assistant Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I207,Time to Work Employment Service (TWES),Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations |National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Time to Work Employment Service (TWES) is a national, voluntary in-prison employment service that assists sentenced Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples to access the support they need to better prepare them to find employment and reintegrate into the community upon their release from prison. The Department of Employment and Workplace Relations will undertake public consultation during 2023 on employment supports for First Nations prisoners.",Resourcing: $3.6 million in 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations|Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Terminating on 30 June 2024 in non-remote areas and aligning with CDP contracts in remote areas,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,x,,,
I208,Yarrabah Employment Services,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Yarrabah Employment Services in Far North Queensland commenced in July 2018 to deliver a place-based approach to employment services.,Resourcing: $1.3 million each year (Funding for Yarrabah Employment Services Pilot is included in the Workforce Australia appropriation) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,Indigenous Area,Queensland,North Queensland,,Yarrabah,,,,,,x,,,,,
I209,Workforce Australia - Self-Employment Assistance,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Workforce Australia - Self-Employment Assistance supports all participants (including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people) to turn a business idea or existing small business into a viable business. Self-Employment Assistance replaced the New Business Assistance with NEIS program in July 2022.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Funding for Self-Employment Assistance is included in the Workforce Australia appropriation,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I210,Workforce Australia,Stakeholder,Commonwealth Government,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Workforce Australia replaced jobactive from 4 July 2022, continuing a comprehensive national network of employment services providers and a digital platform for all Australians in non-remote areas.",Resourcing: $6.2 billion from 2023-24 to 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I211,ParentsNext,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Government has announced an intention to abolish the ParentsNext program from 1 July 2024 and develop a replacement voluntary program, with interim changes to be considered as soon as possible to move ParentsNext to voluntary participation.",Resourcing: $507.4 million over 2023-24 to 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I212,New Jobs Program Trial (approach to replace CDP),Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth has committed to replacing CDP (a remote employment and community development service) with a new program with real jobs, proper wages and decent conditions - developed in partnership with First Nations communities. The New Jobs Program Trial will provide funding for up to 200 jobs in targeted CDP regions. This will allow a broader range of organisations the opportunity to develop and test local approaches to inform the new program.",Resourcing: $9.4 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: The New Jobs Program Trial is opening in 2023. The Trial will run until 31 October 2024. The start date for the new program has not yet been decided.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I213,Local Jobs Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Local Jobs Program commenced in late 2020. It now operates in all 51 employment regions across Australia. It brings together expertise, resources and access to funding at the local level to support individuals, businesses and the broader community in each region. The program has been extended to end on 30 June 2025.",Resourcing: $94.6 million over 2023-24 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I214,Indigenous Skills and Employment Program,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Indigenous Skills and Employment Program will connect Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people to jobs, career advancement opportunities, and to new training and job readiness activities. ISEP activities are designed with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and stakeholders to address local needs or gaps in employment support. The program seeks to complement, rather than duplicate, existing mainstream employment services.",Resourcing: Total program funding $42.8 million (ex GST) annually over years 2023-2024 to 2026-2027 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing - With ISEP activities completion expected by EOFY 2027.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I215,Examination of a Closing the Gap policy partnership on economic participation,Initiative,Partnership,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"As an outcome from the Jobs and Skills Summit, the Commonwealth is working with the Coalition of Peaks to examine a Closing the Gap policy partnership on economic participation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.",Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: To Be Confirmed,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I216,Employment White Paper,Commitment,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This will provide a roadmap for Australia to build a bigger, better-trained and more productive workforce - to boost incomes and living standards and create more opportunities for more Australians. The White Paper will explore strategies to improve Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander employment outcomes, including ideas raised during the Jobs and Skills Summit. The White Paper will be informed by the lived experiences of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in the labour market.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Due for completion by Sept 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I218,Disability Employment Services (DES),Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Disability Employment Services (DES) help people with disability, including Indigenous job seekers, to prepare for, find and keep a job. DES Providers are a mix of large, medium and small for-profit and not-for-profit organisations experienced in supporting people with disability, as well as providing assistance to employers to support employees with disability in the workplace. As at 30 June 2022, 3,977 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander youth were receiving DES services. In the 2021-22 financial year, 68 First Nations youth achieved a 26 week education outcome, 731 achieved a 26 week employment outcome and 380 achieved a 52 week employment outcome.","Resourcing: $1,324.6 million (2022-23), $1,355.0 million (2023-24) and $1,381.6 million (2024-25), $1,420.4 million (2025-26) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Current DES Grant Agreement extended until 30 June 2025",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,,,,,
I219,Jobs and Skills Australia Advice (JSA),Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Jobs and Skills Australia's functions include the ability to provide advice regarding opportunities to improve outcomes for cohorts which have historically experienced labour market disadvantage and exclusions, including First Nations Australians. Details on how this will be carried out are to be agreed through JSA's annual work plan.",Resourcing: Jobs and Skills Australia initial annual work plan and the process for future work plan development are yet to be completed. Costs for any proposed studies of specific relevance to Closing the Gap would need to be determined. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations|Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: Dependent on development of JSA's annual work plan,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I220,VET Data,Initiative,Resource,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Through the VET Data Streamlining program the Commonwealth is working with state and territory governments to improve VET data transparency of all students, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, undertaking vocational education and training across Australia. The delay between training occurring and information on that activity being available will reduce to 1-2 months, so changes in the uptake and completion by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people will be visible at national, state and regional levels.",Resourcing: $57.6 million from 2022-23 to 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I221,Quality of Training Delivery,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Revising the Standards for RTOs and development of a VET Workforce Blueprint have been agreed by Commonwealth and state and territory skills ministers. This work will lead to enhancements in the quality of training and the quality of RTOs and will help create an environment within the sector which is more culturally safe for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander learners.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: 2023 to 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I222,Jobs and Skills Councils (JSC),Network,Other,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"JSCs will identify skills and workforce needs for their sectors, map career pathways across education sectors, develop vocational education and training products, support collaboration between industry and training providers to improve training and assessment practice and act as a source of intelligence on issues affecting their industries. This work will improve the quality of training within the sector which is more supportive of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander learners.",Resourcing: Government allocation $402 million as announced in the October 2022-23 Budget (establishment and operations of JSC) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training|Minister for Employment and Workplace Relations Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I223,Indigenous Student Success Program,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Indigenous Student Success Program (ISSP) provides supplementary funding to universities to help First Nations students take on the demands of university and succeed. Universities offer scholarships, tutorial assistance, mentoring, safe cultural spaces and other personal support services to First Nations students using ISSP funding.","Resourcing: In 2023, $75.5 million has been allocated to 40 universities Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I224,Foundation Skills Programs,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Skills for Education and Employment (SEE) program, includes Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander job seekers. The program helps eligible job seekers learn the skills they need to get the job they want by improving their language, reading, writing and maths skills. Changes to the SEE, announced through October 2022 Budget, will deliver more training through flexible place-based models in remote Australia, from July 2023.   Announced in the 2023-24 Budget, the SEE Program will be redesigned expanding eligibility to those who are not registered job seekers, and will be delivered through a mix of national and local solutions to improve access and delivery. The redesigned SEE program will commence on 1 July 2024 with grant funding made available to First Nation Community-Controlled organisations to partner with Registered Training Organisations or Adult Community Education providers to deliver tailored language, literacy, numeracy and digital skills training in community.   Foundation Skills for Your Future (FSFYF) program for currently employed or recently unemployed people not registered with job services; includes option for employers to arrange tailored LLND support for their employees. This will improve literacy rates which will contribute to Target 6.",Resourcing: FSP ($117 million from 2021-22 ($490.3 million total from 2020-21 to 2023-24)) FSFYF ($14 million from 2021-22 ($42 million total from 2020-21 to 2023-24)) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: SEE is Ongoing FSFYF until June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I225,Foundation Skills for Your Future - Remote Community Pilots,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The four pilots are testing ways to improve access to and development of English language, literacy, numeracy and digital skills training for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in remote community. The Remote Community Pilots (the Pilots) are located in the far west coast communities of Ceduna, Koonibba, Scotdesco, Yalata and Oak Valley in South Australia - Djarindjin/ Ardyaloon/ Lombadina and Beagle Bay in the Dampier Peninsula of Western Australia - Tennant Creek in the Northern Territory - Doomadgee in northern Queensland. This will improve literacy rates which will contribute to Target 6.  As part of the 2023-24 Budget the Government agreed to extend the Pilots by 12 months to 30 June 2024 at a cost of up to $3.5 million. The extension of the Pilot will allow the Government to implement a redesigned Skills for Education and Employment (SEE) program which includes specific support for First Nations peoples. From 1 July 2024 grant funding will be made available to First Nation Community-Controlled organisations to partner with Registered Training Organisations or Adult Community Education providers to deliver tailored language, literacy, numeracy and digital skills training in community.",Resourcing: $13.4 million from 2020-21 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: 2020-2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I226,Away from Base Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Away from Base (AFB) funding program provides financial support and assistance to around 3,300 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students to study via mixed mode or distance education.","Resourcing: $14.0 million per year (NIAA component), Services Australia also run a program that is part of a student's ABSTUDY entitlement - targeted funding for regional and remote students Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians|Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The National Indigenous Australian's Agency, the Department of Social Services and Services Australia are working in partnership to carefully consider the evaluation findings and recommendations on the Away from Base.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I227,Australian Apprenticeships programs,Initiative,Program,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Provide Support for Australian Apprenticeships programs. Current Australian Government funded programs include:  -The Australian Apprenticeships Incentive System, which commenced 1 July 2022, replaces previous Commonwealth incentives and subsidies provided to Australian Apprentices and employers. The Australian Apprenticeships Incentive System replaces the now grandfathered Australian Apprenticeships Incentives Program (AAIP), and the Boosting Apprenticeship Commencements (BAC) wage subsidy which closed to new entrants from 30 June 2022. - Apprenticeship services and non-financial supports to lift completions, which the Australian Government is redesigning. The proposed new model will make mentoring more accessible and provide specialist support to apprentices who experience additional barriers to completing, including First Nations apprentices.  - Trade Support Loans (TSL), which is and income-contingent loan available for eligible trade apprentices and trainees. - In 2021-2022, First Nations apprentices made up 6.0 per cent of commencements (16,505) and 5.6 per cent of completions (5,010). Of the total funding, 4.8 per cent of Apprenticeship Incentives funding ($184,275,355) was used to support First Nations Australians.",Resourcing: The Incentives System and AAIP grandfathering arrangements (including BAC and CAC) total investment of $7.5 billion over 5 years from 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I228,5-year National Skills Agreement with States and Territories,Commitment,Agreement,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,x,,,"The Australian Government has committed to work with States and Territories to develop a new 5-year National Skills Agreement (NSA) to support a VET sector that assists Australians to obtain the skills they need to participate and prosper in the economy. The NSA will be guided by a vision and principles agreed by Skills Ministers and endorsed by National Cabinet. A key priority is to ensure that priority groups, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, have access to the education, training and support needed to obtain well-paid secure jobs.",Resourcing: To Be Confirmed Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: To Be Confirmed,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I229,12-month skills Agreement with states and territories,Commitment,Agreement,Department of Employment and Workplace Relations,Commonwealth Government,x,,x,,,"180,000 Fee-Free TAFE and vocational education places from January 2023, with learner supports that are culturally appropriate for priority groups including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.","Resourcing: Joint funding of $1 billion with states and territories, which includes a Commonwealth commitment of $493 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Skills and Training Delivery Timeframe: 2023",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I230,Universities Demand Driven Funding,Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"From 2021, all Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students who live in regional and remote Australia are guaranteed a Commonwealth supported place at a university of their choice, when accepted into their chosen course of study. An eligible university place is a non-designated, bachelor level course at an Australian public university.",Resourcing: $17.1 million (2020-21 to 2023-24) in funding - targeted funding for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I231,"20,000 additional commonwealth supported places 2023-2024",Initiative,Target,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"These additional places will prioritise opportunities for under-represented groups to attend university, including First Nations Australians, people in regional and remote areas, and those who are the first in family to student at university.","Resourcing: $485.5 million over 2022-23 to 2025-26 ($587 million to end of 2027) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Places commencing in 2023 and 2024 (10,000 each year)",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I232,Mission Based Compacts for Universities,Initiative,Other,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Mission-based compacts provide an opportunity for the Commonwealth to focus universities' attention on the Closing the Gap target and articulate how they will contribute to its achievement. The compacts are published annually, and universities are required to report against them, providing a level of accountability.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I233,Higher Education Participation and Partnerships Program (HEPPP),Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The HEPPP funds universities to support students from identified underrepresented backgrounds to access and succeed in higher education.
I234,Regional Partnership Project Pool Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Regional Partnership Project Pool Program funds university and Regional University Centre-led consortia to deliver collaborative outreach initiatives to enable the higher education aspirations of students in regional and remote areas, which may include Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.
I235,Regional University Centres,Initiative,Resource,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Regional University Centres help students in regional and remote areas access higher education without having to leave their community.
I236,First Nations Languages Educators in primary schools,Initiative,Reform,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Government is supporting local First Nations community and school partnerships for teaching First Nations languages. The initiative is being co-designed in partnership with First Languages Australia, the national peak body for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander language centres and programs. In designing the initiative, the Government will be flexible to the language education needs and aspirations of participating communities, and giving consideration to how the initiative can help to build the community controlled sector. The initiative will help lift the uptake of First Nations language learning by young Australians and increase the number and strength of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages being spoken.",Resourcing: $14.1 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: 2025-26,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,x,,,,,,x
I238,Supporting Quality Boarding for Rural and Remote Students : Indigenous Boarding Providers grants program,Initiative,Funding,"National Indigenous Australians Agency, Department of Education, Department of Social Services, Services Australia",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is extending the Indigenous Boarding Providers grants program for an additional one year and will deliver a design review to identify sustainable, systemic reform options.",Resourcing: $21.6 million for one year Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023 to 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I239,Scaling Up Proven Primary Reading Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Up to $25 million for evidence-based approaches to address key drivers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander student education outcomes. This will focus on expanding access to programs that have already proven to be effective in improving primary school reading outcomes.,Resourcing: $25 million over 4 years from 2021-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: 2021-25,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I240,"Research, tools and strategies to boost Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages education",Initiative,Research,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Research, tools and strategies to boost Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages education. The development of support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander language teachers and the teaching of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages to boost the long-term uptake of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages in Australian school classrooms, through 
I241,City-Country,Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"$26 million to incentivise and support large, high performing metropolitan schools to engage in new formal partnerships with remote Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander schools.",Resourcing: $26 million over 2 years from 2022-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: 2022-24,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I242,Building On Country Boarding Schools,Initiative,Resource,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Commonwealth investment will provide more options for local secondary education in remote communities. Funding will be provided in line with community partnerships and aspirations to support boarding options for students in remote communities, through Studio Schools of Australia. Studio Schools of Australia will build a new middle year secondary boarding school, upgrade their existing Yiramalay Studio School, and establish a new Indigenous Education and Research Centre. ",Resourcing: $70.8 million over 5 years from 2021-22 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2025-26,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I243,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Loading element of school recurrent funding,Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This loading provides extra funding, in addition to the SRS base amount, for each Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander student. Students attracting the funding under this loading might also attract funding under other SRS loadings.The amount of extra funding for each student depends on the proportion of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students in the school.  The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander student loading accounts for about 1.8% of Australian Government  recurrent school funding expenditure in 2023. The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander loading is estimated to provide $3.2 billion over 2022 to 2027.","Resourcing: Targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander funding includes the base schooling resource standard per student amounts, plus loadings for additional needs, such as the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander loading which is estimated to provide $3.2 billion over 2022 to 2027. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I244,Expansion of junior ranger activities to 25 additional sites,Initiative,Target,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Commonwealth is expanding junior ranger activities to 25 additional sites and supporting a distance education pilot in five remote sites and review of existing models,Resourcing: $38.4 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I245,National Early Childhood Program for children with disability or developmental concerns (NECP),Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A National Early Childhood Program (NECP) to provide supported playgroups, parent workshops and online information and resources to young children 0-8 years with disability or developmental concerns, and their parents and carers outside of the National Disability Insurance Scheme. Actions include the provision of information and community supports to children and their families and promote program reach and accessibility to target specific cohorts including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children and families. ","Resourcing: $17.9 million over 4 years from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: All services under the NECP commenced by late 2022, with services targeting specific cohorts, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children and their parents and carers to June 2025.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,x,,,,
I246,New early learning teaching model - Early Learning Teaching Pilot (ELTP),Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In 2021, the Commonwealth committed $1.9 million to trial the design, implementation and evaluation of a new early learning teaching model that strengthens literacy and numeracy learning through explicit instruction with a focus on the participation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children. Three services have been engaged to participate in the ELTP - two in the Northern Territory and one in Victoria. Delivery and commencement of in-service activity and training commenced in 2022.",Resourcing: $1.9 million over 4 years from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education|Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: The pilot will run for the duration of the school year from January 2023 with the final evaluation report to be finalised in December 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I247,Listen to Learn,Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Listen to Learn Service was piloted in 4 schools in metropolitan NSW in December 2022. The schools were nominated by the NSW Education Department and referral pathways were established with the local Community Controlled Health Service. Over 500 children were assessed and some 200 educators completed the online training packages. Initial review of the processes and data identified some process improvements that will be adopted in the next round of screenings. Feedback on the training for educators was extremely positive with the majority of attendees showing significant improvement in their knowledge of how to identify and manage children with hearing loss in the classroom on pre-and post-training assessments. Our processes ensured speedy referral and access to rehabilitation services for 3 children identified as potentially having permanent hearing loss.,Resourcing: $3.0 million over 2 years Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Completion of the program to be extended to 31 December 2023 using existing funding. Rollout to schools and early childhood education services nationally will commence from March 2023. Test procedures have been modified based on review of the pilot sites. We are formalising the extension of the program to the end of December 2023 with the Department of Health. This will allow us to maximise participation by schools and to fully assess the impact of the program.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I248,First Nations Playgroups,Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Commonwealth is resourcing increased support for playgroups and toy libraries across Australia. The measure will allocate dedicated funding to deliver a pilot program to test approaches to First Nations playgroups at 10 new locations. This pilot will be delivered in partnership with Aboriginal community controlled organisations.,Resourcing: $12.4 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-23 to 2025-26,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I249,Additional Child Care Subsidy (ACCS),Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The ACCS is part of the Child Care Safety Net, to give the most vulnerable and disadvantaged children, as well as those from regional and remote communities, a strong start through access to quality early childhood education and care",Resourcing: Targeted funding (general - as needed) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I250,Footprints in Time the Longitudinal Study of Indigenous Children (LSIC),Initiative,Research,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"LSIC is a study of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children and young people across Australia, collecting quantitative and qualitative data to provide insight into how a child's early years affect their development. By tracking children and young people over time, the study can determine factors that give Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children the best start in life to grow up strong, helping to inform policy and programs. The study is guided by the Footprints in Time Steering Committee. The Steering Committee is Indigenous-led and has majority Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander representation",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I252,Providing child care in remote communities through the Community Child Care Fund Restricted (CCCF-R),Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is committed to expanding the Community Child Care Fund Restricted (CCCF-R) program to fund additional and primarily Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander-run child care services in remote and very remote communities. In particular for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children, with the expansion supporting up to 3,500 children.","Resourcing: $268.8 million over four years from 2022-23 in administered funding for the ongoing expenses of the CCCF-R program, which includes funding for the CCCF-R expansion Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I253,Preschool Reform Agreement Commitment,Commitment,Agreement,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Preschool funding arrangements through the Preschool Reform Agreement: Commitment to ongoing funding facilitates access to quality preschool services through the Preschool Reform Agreement with states and territories. This provides funding for all children, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children, to access 15 hours per week (or 600 hours per year) of preschool in the year before school. Work will also focus on improving participation and outcomes for children under the Agreement. Appendices to the Agreement were signed by Education Ministers in late 2022, outlining arrangements to collect data on enrolments and attendance and to trial a new outcomes measure. Implementation Plans for 2023 are to be agreed in early 2023.",Resourcing: $2 billion available over four years from 2021-22 to 2024-25 and $33.6 million to improve the quality and transparency of preschool data and to develop a new Preschool Performance Framework Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: Implementation Plans for 2023 are to be agreed in early 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I254,Plan for Cheaper Childcare - Child Care Subsidy (CCS),Initiative,Funding,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The CCS supports children to attend quality early childhood education and care. Families will receive an increased subsidy from July 2023 when the maximum CCS rate will lift to 90% for families earning under $80,000 for their first child in care. CCS rates will also increase for every family earning less than $530,000. Families with two or more children in care will continue to receive higher rates for their second and younger children. Also from July 2023, the Government will provide a base entitlement of 36 hours of subsidised care per fortnight for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children.","Resourcing: Estimated total spend for Child Care Subsidy in 2022-23 is $10.6 billion. 
I255,Activity test changes for First Nations children,Initiative,Reform,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The changes seek to support access for First Nations children up to two days of subsidised early learning per week, which aims to reduce developmental vulnerability and ensure school readiness.",Resourcing: $33.7 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education Minister for Education Delivery Timeframe: Phase 1 - 10 July 2023 and Phase 2 - 8 July 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I256,Intensive early childhood education and care model trial,Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In 2021, the Commonwealth committed $9.5 million towards an intensive early childhood education and care model trial. This measure is time-limited and will run from 2022-23 to 2025-26. The model will be trialled at a total of four services with the aim of lifting school readiness, including cognitive skills, resilience, and socio-emotional development for vulnerable and disadvantaged children, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children. The trial is a multi-disciplinary model that offers high quality, intensive early education and care, infant mental health and wrap-around family support, in partnership with families and local community organisations and agencies.",Resourcing: $9.5 million over 4 years from 2021-22. Additional philanthropic funding and state and territory support will also support the program Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: 2025-26,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I257,National Children's Education and Care National Workforce Strategy 'Shaping Our Future',Commitment,Strategy,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"All governments, in partnership with the early childhood sector, have finalised a new Children's Education and Care Workforce Strategy. It focusses on building the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander workforce in the sector. An Implementation and Evaluation Plan to guide collaboration between all stakeholders to achieve the National Children's Education and Care Workforce Strategy's action items was developed by the Australian Children's Education and Care Quality Authority (ACECQA) and published in August 2022. Separate work being undertaken by SNAICC focussing on strengthening ACCO's.",Resourcing: $2 million in joint funding with the Commonwealth Government and states and territories has been committed to undertake five implementation projects (Australian Government contribution is 50%) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: 2031,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I258,Inclusion Support Program (ISP),Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Available to Child Care Subsidy approved early childhood education and care services nationally, to deliver more inclusive environments for children with additional needs. The ISP aims to give the most vulnerable and disadvantaged children a strong start, while supporting parents into work. During 2023 there will be a review of ISP performance against effectiveness, efficiency and strategic policy alignment. Review findings will inform the Productivity Commission Inquiry into Child Care and Early Childhood Learning and the whole-of-government Early Years Strategy.","Resourcing: Approximately $133 million per year, targeted funding (general, as needed) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I259,Connected Beginnings Program (DOE),Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This program draws upon the strength and knowledge of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities to improve access to existing early childhood, maternal and child health and family support services to ensure children are safe, healthy and ready to thrive at school by the age of five. The Commonwealth has committed to expand the Connected Beginnings program to 50 sites by 2024-25. Once expanded the program has the potential to support up to 20 per cent of all Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children.","Resourcing: $52.3 million over four years from 2022-23 in administered funding for the ongoing expenses of the Connected Beginnings program, with an additional $51.3 million over the same period committed to the Connected Beginnings expansion program, totalling $103.7 million in funding over the forward estimates Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Early Childhood Education | Minister for Education | Minister for Youth Delivery Timeframe: New sites will be progressively rolled out to June 2025",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I260,Australian Competition and Consumer Commission Childcare Inquiry,Initiative,Other,Treasury,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,In October 2022 the Government directed the ACCC to conduct an inquiry into the market for the supply of childcare services. The inquiry commenced on 1 January 2023 and will provide a final report by 31 December 2023. As part of this inquiry the ACCC will engage with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families on their experiences with the sector. The Direction from Government instructs us to consider the impacts geographical location (including regional and remote areas) and the age and characteristics of the child in care (among other things) have on costs and prices.,Resourcing: $10.8 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Treasurer Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 31 December 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I261,10 Year Generational Strategy for Children and Families in the Northern Territory,Commitment,Strategy,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In 2023, the Children and Families Tripartite Forum will oversee the development and implementation of the first of three Action Plans for the implementation of the Kids Safe, Family Together, Community Strong 10-Year Generational Strategy for Children and Families in the Northern Territory. The Tripartite Forum seeks to broaden governance of the Northern Territory Reform agenda in 2023 through establishing mechanisms to design, implement, monitor and evaluate the components of the Generational Strategy.",Resourcing: DSS provided $1 million to support the work of the Tripartite Forum in contributing to the implementation of the 10 year Generational Strategy Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians | Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Official launch of the Generational Strategy in first quarter 2023 and the Tripartite Forum will develop and implement the first of three action plans in 2023,State,Northern Territory,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I262,Having a Baby Life Event service,Initiative,Program,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The service supported approximately 5260 newborns (of which approx. 5.04% were Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander newborns) from three pilot hospitals (Centenary Hospital for Women and Children in the ACT, Townsville and Gold Coast University Health Services in QLD) to be enrolled in Medicare, through use of birth data from the hospital, removing the need for parents to lodge a newborn enrolment form. The service also includes creation of a myHealth record, Individual Healthcare Identifier and Immunisation Registration for the newborn. Since March 2022, the service has supported proof of birth for Centrelink family assistance claims for 140 newborns in total from across the population, removing the need for parents to lodge a newborn enrolment form. Engagement and co-design activities with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families and relevant government/service providers/advocacy groups on the design of the service has commenced with a co-design workshop held in Darwin in September 2022.","Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services Delivery Timeframe: In 2022-23 financial year, MyGov functionality was incorporated into the service in September 2022 to enable more birth mothers to participate. The MyGov task enables participants who have been discharged from hospital without naming their baby to provide their baby's name at a later date through MyGov. The Birth Registration solution of the Having a Baby Life Event service will delivered in 2023. An evaluation of the pilot has commenced and will be completed in February 2023.A draft national rollout plan has been developed for the service in collaboration with state and territory jurisdictions. The service will be expanded to on-board more hospitals and jurisdictions, and to reach more cohorts (such as rural/remote Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander birth parents).",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I263,Strengthening Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Alcohol and Other Drugs Treatment Services.,Initiative,Capacity Building,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This investment will increase access to enhanced outcomes-focused alcohol and other drugs treatment services; through boosting service delivery, enabling infrastructure improvements, strengthening the capacity of the AOD workforce and improving data collection and reporting. Direction of the investment informed by a sector consultation process.",Resourcing: Up to $66 million to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I264,Preventing harms for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people from Alcohol and Other Substance Misuse,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Alcohol and Other Drugs (AOD) Treatment Services under the Indigenous Advancement Strategy (IAS) currently supports  90 AOD treatment activities including AOD prevention, early intervention, education, treatment, residential rehabilitation and aftercare services for First Nations people.  Investment in Low Aromatic Fuel, capacity building and supply reduction activities to reduce volatile substance use including petrol sniffing in regional and remote communities.",Resourcing: Funding of around $70 million per year is provided under the IAS for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander AOD treatment and support services.   The Australian Government has committed $37.15 million through the IAS over three years to June 2025 for the supply of low aromatic fuel through BP Australia and Viva Energy Australia. The IAS also supports a range of other volatile substance use supply reduction and capacity building activities targeted at harmful volatile substance use ($2.5 million in 2023-24). Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: AOD service providers are delivering AOD services for Indigenous clients and communities under their current contractual requirements.   All existing AOD activities are due for review as at 30 June 2023.  AOD funding post 1 July 2023 will be considered by relevant delegates in the coming months.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I266,"Stronger Places, Stronger People (SPSP)",Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Stronger Places, Stronger People (SPSP) is a community-led, collective impact initiative, stewarded by the Australian Government in partnership with state and territory governments and 10 communities across Australia. It seeks to demonstrate how place based, collective impact can support better outcomes for children and their families through locally tailored and evidence-driven solutions to local problems, in partnership with local people. The long-term goal is to disrupt disadvantage in the participating communities.",Resourcing: Initial commitment of up to $35 million from 2018-19 over five years until 30 June 2024.  A further $64 million has been committed through the 2023-24 Budget to 30 June 2029. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2029,National,N/A,,,,x,x,x,x,,,x,,,,
I267,Services Australia - Provision of Remote Service,Initiative,Program,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Continuation measures under the 2020-21 Budget to deliver government payments and services in remote locations, particularly in the Northern Territory, Western Australia, Northern Queensland, South Australia and Tasmania",Resourcing: $99.3 million over 4 years from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services Delivery Timeframe: A project team is in place to progress the recommendations of the Remote Services MDT and will deliver the new Services Australia Remote Services Model in quarter 1 of 2023/24. By taking an iterative approach to the development of a number of service adjustments may be made during 2022/23.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
I268,Women's Leadership and Development Program,Initiative,Program,Department of the Prime Minister & Cabinet,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Women's Leadership and Development Program provides grants aimed at improving the economic security, employment, leadership and safety outcomes for women in Australia. This includes a range of projects working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women. WLDP funding is also provided to the National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Women's Alliance (NATSIWA), as one of six National Women's Alliances. NATSIWA play a key role engaging with the community and working with government to elevate the voices of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander women to inform policy development.","Resourcing: $10,299,991? over 2020-21 to 2025-26 is currently committed to projects specifically designed to work with First Nations women. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Women Delivery Timeframe: Individual project timeframes vary with completion ranging between 2022-23 to 2025-26.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I269,Australia has worked to mainstream language on gender equality and Indigenous women's rights issues by regularly proposing amendments to protect and enhance language in resolutions on Indigenous women's rights.,Initiative,Other,Department of the Prime Minister & Cabinet|National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,," Australia has worked to mainstream language on gender equality and Indigenous women's rights issues by regularly proposing amendments to protect and enhance language in resolutions on Indigenous women's rights. This includes our commitments to the UN Convention on the Elimination of Discrimination Against Women (CEDAW), supporting/engaging with civil society organisations (such as NATSIWA) on the UN Commission on the Status of Women (CSW) and providing inputs on intersections between Indigenous peoples and gender issues in the Human Rights Council (HRC).",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Women Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I270,Support innovation in the community-controlled sector,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Support innovation to increase, maximise and improve the current disability services and support capacity. (Disability Sector Strengthening Plan).",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I271,Information and communication systems used by community-controlled sector are accessible,Initiative,Target,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Ensure information and communication systems, including IT infrastructure used by community-controlled sector are accessible. (Disability Sector Strengthening Plan).",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I272,Increase ACCO / ACCHOs service offer to deliver disability services,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,By enhancing and supporting ACCO / ACCHOs organisational infrastructure needs.(Disability Sector Strengthening Plan),Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I273,First Peoples Disability Network,Stakeholder,NGO,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,First Peoples Disability Network to continue to be funded to provide systemic advocacy as the Disability Representative Organisation for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with disability.,Resourcing: $0.36 million per year (2023-24) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I275,Community-controlled organisations and buildings meet accessibility standards,Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Ensure all new community-controlled infrastructure and upgrades to existing infrastructure meet accessibility and inclusion requirements (Premise Standards and Reviews). (Disability Sector Strengthening Plan),Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I276,Support an increase in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander individual community advocates,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,, The Department of Social Services will pilot for 12 months the provision of targeted individual advocacy supports targeting First Nations people with disability. The pilot will comprise funding for 13 specialist Indigenous Community Advocates under the current National Disability Advocacy Program (NDAP). Advocates will be placed within eight existing NDAP providers and one new NDAP provider.,Resourcing: $1.835 million total in 2022-23 and 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The Pilot will run from 1 July 2023 to 30 June 2024.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I278,Continue to implement the Younger People in Residential Aged Care (YPIRAC) targets,Initiative,Target, Department of Social Services Department of Health and Aged Care National Disability Insurance Agency ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Continue to implement YPIRAC targets as per Recommendation 74 of the Aged Care Royal Commission Final Report to reduce the number of younger Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people living in aged care accommodation, unless there are exceptional circumstances.","Resourcing: As part of the 2023-24 Budget, the Australian Government announced $7.3 million in funding to further reduce the number of people under the age of 65 living in RAC, including $3.6 million for DoHAC Education and training package for clinical staff and YPIRAC representatives,  $2.4 million for DoHAC centralised eligibility decision making for younger people seeking entry to RAC, and $1.3 million for the DSS evaluation of YPIRAC initiatives to date to inform future initiatives.  In December 2021, the Commonwealth announced additional funding of approximately $16 million (Department of Health) to extend the current YPIRAC Coordinator Program until the end of 2025. The YPIRAC Coordinator Program originally allocated $10.1 million as part of the Budget 2020-21 with the intent to engage with younger people who are not eligible or have not tested their eligibility for the NDIS, living in, or at risk of entering residential aged care, and help them navigate various state and federal government systems
I279,Improving ear health and hearing outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children,Initiative,Target,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Hearing Australia's First Nations Action Plan (2022-2025) is aimed at reducing excessively high rates of ear disease (otitis media) and hearing loss among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children.
I280,Implement and monitor the National Disability Data Asset (NDDA) - Next Phase,Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The NDDA will provide insights into Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with disability that can inform positive change throughout a range of the Closing the Gap 'Priority' and 'Outcome' measures.,"Resourcing: $40 million Commonwealth Funding Contribution Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The 2022-23 financial year will see a continuation of the work undertaken in 2021-22 and include engagement with people with lived experience of disability to inform the final design of the NDDA. In addition, ongoing co-governance arrangements for the NDDA will be established (1 January 2022 to 30 June 2025).",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I281,Grow the First Nations disability workforce,Initiative,Target,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,First Nations Disability Workforce Sector Principles to map existing Disability and First Nations peoples’ workforce strategies and identify specific principles to be inclusive of a First Nations Disability Workforce Sector (Disability Sector Strengthening Plan). ,"Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme|Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: As at 3 January 2023, there are 188 Agents and 88 Access Points in remote and very remote locations.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I282,"First Nations Disability Evidence, Data and Policy Dialogues",Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Across Commonwealth agencies, co-design and co-deliver policy seminars to increase and embed a focus on access, inclusion and disability across all outcomes and targets, defining and identifying institutional racism and discrimination, and scoping activities and policy initiatives to affect positive change within Commonwealth agencies.",Resourcing: 400000 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Complete. The report was delivered in December 2022 with the activation of findings.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I284,Ensure NDIS thin markets projects are culturally appropriate (NDIS Thin Market Projects),Initiative,Capacity Building,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Thin market intervention projects support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander NDIS participants to use the funding allocated in their plans in circumstances where poor market connection, low demand, or other barriers to service provision exist. The projects do this by working extensively with the community, providers and other intermediaries to resolve identified barriers and issues.. In 2023, locations of focus for NDIS market interventions include the APY Lands, the Kimberley, and Longreach. The NDIA has been working with communities, local NDIS providers, state governments and other stakeholders in the APY Lands and the Kimberley to develop these NDIS markets since 2020. This work will continue in 2023, taking a whole-of-community approach, listening and responding to community priorities, and seeking opportunities to integrate across other service systems to build a strong and culturally-relevant NDIS.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I285,Ensure Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with disability continue to be a priority cohort,Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Ensure Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with disability continue to be a priority cohort within the community under the current Information, Linkages and Capacity Building (ILC) program Investment Strategy 2018-24 and in the development of a new ILC strategy.","Resourcing: Funding is not specifically allocated for activities that target Indigenous Australians with disability; rather, Indigenous Australians are regarded as a priority cohort in the current ILC grants program which aims to create more inclusive services, communities and workplaces. Currently active grants as at 31 March 2023 include 45 (with total funding of $41.5 million) that include Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with disability as a priority cohort. These projects range from 1 to 4 years in length. Total funding for such projects could vary in future rounds, depending on the particular ILC round and focus of the applications received. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme|Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The future ILC strategy will incorporate findings of the Disability Royal Commission and NDIS Review, slated to report in September and October 2023 respectively. These reviews consulted widely. Should further targeted codesign be needed to finalise the ILC strategy, the views of people with disability, including First Nations peoples and their representative organisations, will be sought prior to submission to disability ministers.  In line with Australia's Disability Strategy, First Nations peoples  will continue to be among priority cohorts for the delivery of the ILC.  A  performance measurement framework for ILC grants will be implemented from 2023-24 to support an outcomes focus for future ILC grants including those  targeting First Nations peoples with disability.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I286,Cultural Model of Inclusion,Commitment,Framework,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Develop and implement a framework and embed Cultural Model of Inclusion as a governance structure within community-controlled organisations, including development and implementation of an Organisational Assessment Tool (Disability Sector Strengthening Plan).",Resourcing: 400000 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2022-2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I287,"Counselling and Advocacy Support Services Royal Commission into Violence, Abuse, Neglect and Exploitation of People with Disability",Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Counselling and Advocacy Support Services Royal Commission into Violence, Abuse, Neglect and Exploitation of People with Disability","Resourcing: $147 million (2019-23) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: The department will be undertaking an evaluation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people specific advocacy supports across 2022-23 to determine the effectiveness and impact of the supports, and to inform future policy and program decisions. The 2022-23 financial year will see a continuation of the work undertaken across 2021-22. The evaluation has commenced  and will be completed late 2023. ",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I288,Consultation and direct engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in the design and delivery of disability employment supports,Initiative,Other,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Consultation and direct engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in the design and delivery of disability employment supports, including reform to DES",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: A new model will commence 1 July 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I290,Allied Health in Remote Schools Project,Initiative,Program,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,A trial to deliver allied health services to support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students with disability in remote schools to identify and address developmental delay and disabilities that may lead or have led to disengagement and poor learning outcomes,Resourcing: $1.257 million  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 31/07/2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I291,Social and Emotional Wellbeing (Mental Health) Policy Partnership,Initiative,Partnership,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"On 26 August 2022, Joint Council approved the establishment arrangements for the Social and Emotional Wellbeing (Mental Health) Policy Partnership under Priority Reform One of the National Agreement on Closing the Gap. The Partnership is co-led by the Department of Health and Aged Care and the Coalition of the Peak Organisations representative, Gayaa Dhuwi (Proud Spirit) Australia. It includes representatives from Commonwealth, state and territory governments, Coalition of the Peaks and other First Nations organisations. The first full meeting of the Partnership was held in March 2023 and focused on relationship building, creating a shared understanding of the scope and role of the Partnership, and identifying initial priorities.  ",Resourcing: The Social and Emotional Wellbeing (Mental Health) Policy Partnership received $8.6 million over 3 years in the March 2022-23 Budget. $7.3 million is allocated to the Department of Health and Aged Care to fund the Partnership (including establishment) and provide departmental support. $1.3 million is allocated to NIAA for departmental support. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2022-23 to 2024-25 (3 years initial funding),National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I292,Establish five Policy Partnerships (See each policy partnership action for more details) NIAA,Initiative,Target,"Department of Health and Aged Care|National Indigenous Australians Agency|Department of Social Services|Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts|Attorney-General's Department",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Establish five Policy Partnerships,"Resourcing: Justice: $ 7.6 M Early childhood care and development $ 10.2 M Social and emotional wellbeing: $ 8.6 M Housing $ 9.2 M ($ 7.148 M with DSS and NIAA covering resourcing)  and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages $ 11.2 M Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 3 year review to decide on next steps:
I293,Fund NACCHO to provide policy options to government around integrated care models,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"NACCHO will provide policy options to government around integrated care models which would benefit Indigenous Australians, through streamlines funding and regulation requirements around aged care, primary health care, disability, veterans care and mental health.",Resourcing: $1.21 million funding over 9 months to NACCHO (2022-23) for the Care and Support Ready program that aims to assist Aboriginal community-controlled services to enter the aged care and disability support market and reap the benefits of an integrated care service system. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Policy options to be provided to Government in early 2023. Further details on implementation to be determined.,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I295,DOHAC Program Audit,Initiative,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Department of Health and Aged Care will conduct a department-wide audit of programs to identify funding and services delivering health care for First Nations people currently provided by non-Indigenous organisations, which could be better delivered by First Nations led organisations.",Resourcing: Costs of review are to be absorbed within existing resources. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Minister Butler publicly committed to the audit at the NACCHO Annual Conference in October 2022. An initial review will commence in 2022-23 and inform a multi-year transition program to 2026-27.,National,N/A,,,,,x,x,,,x,x,,,,
I296,Data access and sharing,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Department of Health and Aged Care is expanding the range of reports collected through the Health Data Portal (HDP) and made available through the Qlik applications. The next reports to be migrated to the HDP are the Activity Work Plan, Risk Plan and updated Performance Report.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing. The Activity Work Plan and Risk Plan will be collected through the HDP from June 2023. The updated Performance Report will be implemented by September 2023.,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I298,Aged Care Capital Assistance Program (ACCAP),Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Support essential aged care infrastructure to increase access to aged care services, improve quality outcomes and support service sustainability and viability in thin market locations. A key focus is to improve equity of access for First Nations people, senior Australians living in regional, rural and remote locations and those who are homeless or risk of homelessness. Under ACCAP, the department will manage multiple targeted and open grant opportunities using evidence based data to fund critical services in need of infrastructure upgrades.","Resourcing: Up to $529 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26, with at least $149 million from 2026-27 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing from June 2022, with the next grant opportunity expected to open in the first half of 2023.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I299,National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Ageing and Aged Care Council Limited (NATSIAACC) will lead reform priorities,Network,Peak Body,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Ageing and Aged Care Council Limited (NATSIAACC) is an independent, peak body organisation who represent the views of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in aged care and organisations providing aged care services (residential and in-home). NATSIAACC will lead reform priorities for older Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people including:
I300,Delivery of aged care services through Aboriginal Community Controlled Organisations (ACCOs),Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Department is undertaking a process to engage organisations to assist Aboriginal Community Controlled Organisations with governance, business, training and leadership, increasing the capability and viability of these organisations to deliver aged care to First Nations people.","Resourcing: $8 million from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Three capacity building projects have commenced, assisting Aboriginal Community Controlled Organisations (ACCO) in the Perth, Darwin and Western Sydney metro areas. It is expected all three will be progressed to approved provider status by 30 June 2023 increasing the number of ACCOs capable of providing services to First Nations Elders.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I301,Increase delivery of National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Flexible Care (NATSIFAC) Services,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Increase delivery of National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Flexible Care (NATSIFAC) Services, providing additional funding to better support the delivery of quality and culturally safe aged care services to older First Nations people","Resourcing: $61.5 million in additional funding from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: To date, the Department has provided $38m in funding uplifts to the NATSIFAC Services in 2021-22 and 2022-23. Further uplifts we be applied to better support the delivery of quality and culturally safe aged care services to older First Nations people in 2023-24 and 2024-25.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I303,NATSIFAC Program Targeted Infrastructure funding,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Targeted capital investment to enable four National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Flexible Aged Care services to rebuild aged care buildings, supporting culturally safe care to older First Nations people, enabling them to enjoy long and healthy lives. This funding is provided through the Aged Care Capital Assistance Program.","Resourcing: $115 million over four years to rebuild ageing infrastructure to better support culturally safe care for First Nations elders Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: A 2021-22 Grant Opportunity resulted in entering into grant agreements with four NATSIFAC Services to years to rebuild ageing infrastructure  in Port Augusta SA, Morningside Qld, Wadeye and Kaltukatjara NT. Rebuilds are expected to be completed by 30 June 2025. ",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I304,Ear and Hearing Health Partnership Committee (EPC),Network,Committee,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Ear and Hearing Health Partnership Committee (EPC) was established in December 2021 for an initial period of three years to reform hearing programs to be more effective for First Nations people. This partnership between the Commonwealth, states and territories and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representatives in the hearing health sector aims to co-design changes to IAHP programs to transition ear health promotion, community engagement functions and training and workforce development coordination to community-controlled organisations.","Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health Delivery Timeframe: Over the 2021-2024 period, the Ears Partnership Committee aims to provide a culturally safe, shared decision-making structure through which to identify and drive reforms to significantly enhance Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ear and hearing health. The Committee will input to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ear and hearing health National strategy (led by NACCHO), which will be finalised in 2023.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I305,Improve the hearing health of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Improve the hearing health of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people through additional and tailored hearing services under the Community Service Obligations (CSO) component of the Australian Government Hearing Services Program provided by Hearing Australia. NHMRC TCR into Hearing Health 2021: evidence based services funded 3 grants that focus on improving models of care and hearing health services for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.  ,"Resourcing: CSO is funded by annual capped budget and supports a range of eligible cohorts. In 2022-23, the CSO budget is $84.2million. Funding is indexed annually.  NHMRC TCR funding of $3,715,81 from 2022-25 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I306,Leverage the Younger People in Residential Aged Care (YPIRAC) target of no people under the age of 65 living in residential aged care by 1 January 2025,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Leverage the Younger People in Residential Aged Care (YPIRAC) target to reduce the number of younger Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people with a disability living in aged care accommodation. The Department of Health and Aged Care, Department of Social Services, and the National Disability Insurance Agency are working together to achieve the YPIRAC target, to reduce the number of younger people under the age of 65 living in, or at risk of entering, residential aged care, apart from in exceptional circumstances. Choice will be provided to First Nations people between 50 and 64 years of age who are eligible for the aged care system, by supporting them to find alternative age-appropriate options with the supports they need, where they indicate their preference is not to remain in residential aged care.","Resourcing: $26.6 million from 2020-21 to 2023-25 (Department of Health and Aged Care) for System Coordinators to actively engage with younger people living in, or at risk of entering residential aged care, and help them navigate various state and federal government systems; and commission the Australian Institute of Health and Welfare to undertake public reporting about younger people living in residential aged care.  $7.3 million from 2023-24 to 2025-26 for a package of initiatives to further reduce the number of people under the age of 65 living in residential aged care. Funding includes: • $3.6 million over two years from 2023-24 ($3 million for the Department of Health and Aged Care and $0.6 million for the Department of Social Services) to provide targeted education and training packages for general practitioners (GPs), clinical staff, social workers, carers, advocates, families, and other organisations and people that support and influence the decision making of younger people in residential aged care. • $2.4 million over three years from 2023-24 for the Department of Health and Aged Care to  establish a central function to support nationally consistent decision making on the eligibility of younger people seeking to enter residential aged care, ensuring it is only ever a last resort. • $1.3 million in 2023-24 for the Department of Social Services to evaluate actions already taken by governments to reduce the number of YPIRAC, to inform future initiatives. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Aged Care|Minister for Health and Aged Care|Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme|Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: YPIRAC System Coordinators to be completed by 31 December 2025.  DSS evaluation to be completed by 30 June 2024.  Targeted education and training packages to be completed by 30 June 2025.   Strengthened entry requirements for residential aged care  to be completed by 30 June 2026.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I307,Sport4All pilot project,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This is being expanded into a nationwide program which includes a specific focus on increasing participation of First Nations people with disability in sport and physical activity through local schools and sporting clubs. The program expansion will see an additional 80 Inclusion Coaches employed in identified Local Government Areas, that will engage approximately 53,000 First Nations people with a disability.",Resourcing: $10.291 million over 4 years from 2022-2 and new funding announced as an election commitment in 2022 and confirmed at the October 2022 Federal Budget Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Minister for Sport Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I308,Trusted Indigenous Facilitator Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Work in collaboration with the National Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation (NACCHO) to support a staged roll-out of the Trusted Indigenous Facilitator Program, engaging 250 people to provide trusted face-to-face support and assist older First Nations people and families better navigate and access care.",Resourcing: $106 million from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: A contract for services with NACCHO to deliver the Trusted Indigenous Facilitator program was executed on 22 June 2022. The first tranche of Trusted Indigenous Facilitators will commence facilitation services in April 2023 with all 250 expected to be implemented across the country by July 2024.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I309,Additional Aboriginal Community-Controlled Health Service sector funding for improved infrastructure (Existing),Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To address seriously deteriorating or non-existent health infrastructure, the Commonwealth will make an investment to deliver new and renovated health clinics and associated housing for health professionals.","Resourcing: $254.4 million from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health Delivery Timeframe: Funding expended by 30 June 2025.   The Closing the Gap (CtG) Major Capital Works Program (Round 1) grant opportunity has been finalised. A total of 61 projects totalling $138.61 million have been approved for funding. . Round 2 is anticipated to open mid 2023 and will offer $70m.   The 2022-23 Service Maintenance Program  will offer $10 million in funding for repairs, maintenance and upgrades to eligible ACCHS, with funding to be expended in the 2023-24 financial year.  The 2022-23 SMP grant opportunity closed on 27 April 2023 and is now under assessment. ",National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I310,AFL boosting physical activity in First Nations communities in Northern Territory,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The AFL is being supported to boost physical activity amongst First Nations communities in the Northern Territory. The program will focus on increasing participation amongst Indigenous school-aged women and girls across the NT in AFL training and competitions, to promote the physical and mental health benefits of participating in sport.",Resourcing: $2.2 million over three years from 2022-23 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Minister for Sport Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 31 December 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I311,Allied Health Case Conferencing MBS items,Initiative,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Medicare Benefits Schedule (MBS) items have been created to reimburse allied health professionals for participating in multidisciplinary case conferencing. This action will assist in improved care coordination and deliver better health outcomes to First Nations peoples, who may have multiple care providers and/or complex needs. The new items are for eligible allied health professionals participating in multidisciplinary case conferences for people with chronic disease under the care of a General Practitioner as part of Team Care Arrangements, as well as patients aged under 13 years (under 25 years from 1 March 2024) under the care of a specialist, consultant physician or GP to provide early diagnosis and treatment of autism or any other pervasive developmental disorders.",Resourcing: $13.7 million from 2021-22 to establish items Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completed and live by November 2021  (change to eligible age from 1 March 2024),National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I312,Additional Aboriginal Community-Controlled Health Service sector funding for improved infrastructure (New),Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is accelerating efforts to close the gap in health and wellbeing outcomes through additional infrastructure investment in areas of large and growing First Nations populations. This will support improved access to culturally safe, inclusive, comprehensive health care services for First Nations people.",Resourcing: $184.43 million from 2022- 23 to 2025-26 (includes $22 million for the Birthing on Country Centre of Excellence) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health Delivery Timeframe: Funding expended by June 2026.   Agreements have been offered to 18 First Nations organisations (26 projects) . ,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I313,Better Renal Services for First Nations Peoples,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Establishment of up to 30 dialysis units to help First Nations people in regional and remote locations with end stage kidney disease. Projects will include upgrades to existing dialysis units, small scale water infrastructure and the build of new dialysis units. The measure will improve the health of First Nations peoples with end-stage kidney disease who must undergo dialysis treatment, often in locations far from their communities.",Resourcing: $45 million over four years (2022-23 to 2025-26).  An additional $28.2 million (2023-2026) was announced in Budget 2023-24.  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: This measure will be delivered over four years 2022-23 to 2025-26. The first 6 sites were announced  on 13 April 2023.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I314,Blood Borne Viruses (BBV) and Sexually Transmissible Infections (STI) Research Program,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Blood Borne Viruses (BBV) and Sexually Transmissible Infections (STI) Research Program to develop evidence-based policy for managing and preventing BBV and STIs, with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people a priority population. The research program further supports the Fifth National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Blood Borne Viruses and Sexually Transmissible Infections (BBV STI) Strategy.",Resourcing: BBV STI Research has allocated grants worth up to $4.8 million per year from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I316,Dialysis Treatment Buses in Far West NSW,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Provision of two dialysis treatment buses to the Far West NSW region to support end stage kidney disease patients to access dialysis treatment without the need to travel significant distances, saving patients significant financial and time costs.",Resourcing: $1.1 million in 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Delivery has been delayed until 2023-24 due to community consultation on preferred options. ,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I317,Enhanced Response to the outbreak of infectious syphilis in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities,Initiative,Reform,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Enhanced Response to the outbreak of infectious syphilis in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities in Queensland, the Northern Territory, Western Australia and South Australia, which includes funding for ACCHOs for workforce supplementation, training and syphilis point-of-care-testing.",Resourcing: The Enhanced Response was provided $21.1 million from 2017-18 to 2020-21 and recently was provided approximately $24 million from 2021-22 to 2023-24 from the Indigenous Australians Health Programme. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I318,Enhancing the National Cancer Screening Register,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Enhancing the National Cancer Screening Register to facilitate implementation of the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program alternative access to kits model. This access model will facilitate the direct distribution of bowel screening kits to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people (an under screened cohort of the program) through Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander primary health care centres.,"Resourcing: $12 million in the 2021-22 Budget has been used to successfully enhance the National Cancer Screening Register  to facilitate the direct distribution of National Bowel Cancer Screening Program bowel screening kits to eligible participants through primary health care centres, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. The ongoing implementation of this approach to kit distribution is funded through the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I319,Establishment of governance group to oversee the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander GP Training Salary Support Program,Network,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Establishment of governance group to oversee the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander GP Training Salary Support Program and provide advice on other elements of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander GP Training to ensure Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are part of future decision-making, and that the program settings are supporting the Australian Government's Closing the Gap health targets. Members of the First Nations GP Training Committee (GPTC) will be appointed by the Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health. Ministerial approval for an Expression of Interest (EOI) process through relevant sector organisations has been received.",Resourcing: Funding of $38m per year allocated to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health components of the Australian General Practice Training Program. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health Delivery Timeframe: It is planned that the new Committee will meet for the first time before July 2023,National,N/A,,,,x,,x,,,,,,,,
I320,Expansion of the Allied Health Rural Generalist Pathway (AHRGP) and Allied Health Assistance Workforce Package.,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The program aims to equip qualified allied health professionals for practice in rural and remote Australia and improves allied health professionals' capability, competence and confidence resulting in improving the health outcomes of rural Australians. While the program is not Indigenous -specific, it does have specific targets for participation by First Nations allied health professionals on the program. These targets are to ensure that First Nations allied health professionals have access to advanced study opportunities.",Resourcing: $9.51 million from 2021-2022 to 2023-2024 (note this is total program funding) Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion by February 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I322,Formal Funding Agreement with NACCHO through Sector Support Network,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Sector Support Network grant aims to build on existing arrangements with the National Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation (NACCHO) and the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Sector Support Organisations (SSOs) to align programs and service delivery of the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services (ACCHSs) with broader health reforms for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. This includes, but is not limited to ensuring implementation is in line with current and emerging direction under the -National Agreement on Closing the Gap (National Agreement) - National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Plan 2021-2031 (Health Plan) - National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workforce Strategic Framework and Implementation Plan 2021-2031 and other key health policy reforms such as the 10-year Primary Health Care Plan and the 10-year National Preventive Health Strategy.",Resourcing: $94.3 million over four years from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: This Grant is for an initial period of four years from 1 July 2022 to 30 June 2026. A two-year extension option under a rolling funding model may be considered on a biennial basis based on performance.,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I323,Funding 500 First Nations health workers,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Program will support up to 500 First Nations trainees to undertake Certificate III or IV accredited training to enable them to work across various health settings and be able to deliver culturally appropriate care to First Nations peoples. The program comprises two stages, an establishment phase that will prepare for the roll out of trainees and include the development of resources, recruitment, establishing governance structures, program planning and logistics to enable trainees to commence in 2023-24 financial year. Grant Opportunity Guidelines are being developed prior to the roll out of trainees. The second stage, delivery phase will commence from 1 July 2023 until the end of the program.",Resourcing: $54.3 million from 2021-22  to 2026-27  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Anticipated start date for traineeships is 1 July 2023 and completion due by 30 June 2027,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I324,Funding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Professional Organisations (ATSIHPOs),Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Funding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Professional Organisations (ATSIHPOs) to support health systems to be more appropriate and accessible for First Nations peoples, including those living in rural and remote areas. This work involves growing the number of First Nations people within the workforce, increasing the cultural capability of the broader health workforce and supporting better care of First Nations peoples.",Resourcing: $52.9 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,x,x,,,,,,,,
I325,"Health outcomes are improved by involving Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities in risk management planning for water on Country used for recreational, cultural and spiritual purposes.",Initiative,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,A risk management framework is being drafted for inclusion in the updated Guidelines for Managing Risks in Recreational Water. The draft framework specifically recommends actively involving First Nations communities in risk management planning to ensure their voices and traditional knowledge is acknowledged and considered when keeping people safe from water on Country.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Public consultation on the draft guidelines and framework is anticipated in early 2024. NHMRC will be seeking feedback on the draft Guidelines from interested Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander stakeholders and representatives. Publication of the Guidelines is anticipated in early 2025.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I326,Implement the National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workforce Strategic Framework and Implementation Plan 2021-2031 (Workforce Plan).,Commitment,Strategy,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Implement the National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workforce Strategic Framework and Implementation Plan 2021-2031 (Workforce Plan).,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I327,Implementation of the Department of Health and Aged Care's Closing the Gap Action Plan to lead structural change required under Priority Reforms,Commitment,Action Plan,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Implementation of the Department of Health and Aged Care's Closing the Gap Action Plan to lead structural change required under Priority Reforms,Resourcing: Focus on re-prioritising current and future expenditure to address First Nations' health Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I328,Improve cultural safety across the healthy system - Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT),Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The RHMT Program provides funding to universities to encourage the recruitment and retention of rural and remote health professionals. RHMT program funded universities are required to ensure that all health students are given appropriate cultural training prior to undertaking placements in First Nations communities and to report on the number of students undertaking this training prior to clinical placement in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community settings.,Resourcing: $210 million a year to 21 universities under the 2022-24 RHMT Grant Agreements Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health|Assistant Minister for Rural and Regional Health Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I329,Improving Renal and Cardiac care for First Nations peoples,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Upgrades and purchase of medical equipment to improve the quality of care, and health and wellbeing outcomes for First Nations people to help prevent and manage chronic disease.","Resourcing: $740,000 (ex GST) 2022-23 and $220,000 to Redfern AMS and $520,000 to TAC AMS Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Funding to be provided within the 2022-23",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I330,Increase Primary Health Networks (PHNs) commissioning of alcohol and other drugs (AOD) treatment services for First Nations people,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Under the Government's National Ice Action Strategy (NIAS) PHNs receive specific funding to commission AOD treatment services in their regions for First Nations people.,Resourcing: $41.5 million from 2022-23 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing (The current PHN AOD Schedule is in place until 30 June 2024),National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I331,Indigenous Health Workforce Traineeship Program (IHWT),Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Indigenous Health Workforce Traineeships (IHWT) program provides funding to approved National Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation (NACCHO) affiliates, or equivalent organisations to -increase the number of skilled Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people working in the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander primary health care sector - ?create viable career pathways in health for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people - build the capacity of Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services (ACCHSs) to provide culturally appropriate health care to its Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander clients.",Resourcing: $13.6 million from 2020-21 to 2022-23 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by 31 December 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I332,Indigenous media strategy and outreach program to increase uptake of digital health technologies,Commitment,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Australian Digital Health Agency's Communications and Marketing Team to perform an audit of our media database to ensure comprehensive coverage of First Nations communities. Develop an Indigenous media strategy (objectives/messages/media targeting) and implement an ongoing media outreach program aimed at driving awareness and uptake of key digital health technology.,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Audit and Strategy to be delivered by June 2023 and Delivery to commence July 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I333,Indigenous Medical Specialist Trainees Program (IMST) - now known as the Specialist Trainee Support Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Australian Indigenous Doctor's Association (AIDA) is funded to provide a Specialist Trainee Support Program that supports Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander doctors who are looking to enter or undertake specialist training. As part of this project, AIDA and the Specialist Medical Colleges collaborate to recruit and retain more Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander specialist trainees. AIDA have supported colleges on the implementation of the 15 agreed standards to grow the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander workforce and progress towards a culturally safe health system.",Resourcing: 934000 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 1 year - 1 July 2023 to 30 June 2024 ,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I336,National Health Academy (Indigenous Allied Health Australia),Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"(indigenous Allied Health Australia (IAHA) is funded for its Health Academy program. This program works with high school students from years 7-12, starting with health literacy, moving to leadership and career planning and transitioning into the Health Academy in Years 11 and 12. IAHA's Health Academies support participants to enter a school-based traineeship pathway, complete a Year 12 qualification, gain work experience and obtain a Certificate III in Allied Health Assistance. Health Academies currently operate in five locations Darwin, South East Queensland, Northern Rivers (New South Wales), Greater Western Sydney and the Australian Capital Territory.",Resourcing: $8 million (ex GST) from 2022-23 to 2025-26 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by 30 June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I337,Prevention and early intervention of rheumatic heart disease,Initiative,Reform,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Expanded treatment and prevention of acute rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease, to be implemented through a partnership approach with the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services sector.","Resourcing: An additional $13.9 million to NACCHO from 2022-2023 to 2024-25, bringing total funding to NACCHO to $31.9 million and total investment in the RFS to $45 million Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Agreement expires 30 June 2025",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I338,Prioritising Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander populations in preventing communicable diseases,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"COVID-19 vaccines, testing and treatments for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander populations, including through partnerships with Aboriginal Community-Controlled Health Services.                                                        The National Immunisation Program (NIP) provides 30 free Vaccines for 17 vaccine preventable communicable disease for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander populations. ",Resourcing: COVID Vaccines: $57.3 million from January 2023 to June 2024             The National Immunisation Program (NIP): $450 million each year  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: COVID Vaccines: Current policy authority to June 2024                       NIP Vaccines - ongoing,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I339,Provision of rapid point-of-care (POC) cervical screening tests,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Provision of rapid point-of-care (POC) cervical screening tests and follow up in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, to help overcome delays in cervical cancer screening and prevention and remove the requirement for women to travel from country to follow up. Aims to increase access and participation in cervical screening for First Nations women, who are generally under-screened and more likely to develop cervical cancer than women who are regular screeners.",Resourcing: $5.9 million from 2022-23 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I340,Reduce rates of smoking among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Ongoing implementation of the Tackling Indigenous Smoking (TIS) program, which aims to improve life expectancy among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples by reducing tobacco use. The Budget 2023 package on vaping measures included an extension of the TIS program to 2026-27 and additional funding of $10m per annum (indexed) to scale up vaping prevention activities nationally. From 1 July 2025, the TIS program will be fully funded out of the Preventative Health and Chronic Disease outcome, which will free up Indigenous specific funding under the Indigenous Australians' Health Program funding for other First Nations Health priorities.",Resourcing: $56.95m per annum (indexed) over four years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: TIS providers will have new grants in place from 1 July 2023 through to 30 June 2026. Approval and funding for TIS to continue through 2026-27 was provided through the 2023 Budget.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I341,Continued investment in research,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Since the program's inception in 2015, and as of 30 April 2023, the Medical Research Future Fund (MRFF) has invested $51.92 million in 24 grants with a focus on First Nations children and maternal health.  Of this amount, $18.4 million has been awarded through the 2021 Improving the Health and Wellbeing of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Mothers and Babies Grant Opportunity, to support First Nations-led projects that translate existing knowledge through culturally safe care for pregnancy, birthing and the post-natal period. Also, $15 million has been provided for a Maternal Health and Healthy Lifestyles grant opportunity for funding commencing 2022-23. This Grant Opportunity will focus on improving the health of pregnant women, mothers, and young children, including a particular those focus First Nations people.  NHMRC Targeted Call for Research - Improving Indigenous Maternal and Child Health in the Early Years (IIMCHEY) 2022 awarded $5 million over 5 years to identify strength-based, action-oriented approaches and interventions that value Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ concepts of health and wellbeing, cultural practices, and knowledge and learning to ensure that all children have the best start to life. The outcomes will improve maternal and child healthcare knowledge, health policies, care models and interventions and create a sustainable, multidisciplinary network extending beyond research conclusion.",Resourcing: $51.92 million from the MRFF  $5 million NHMRC Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: The Medical Research Future Fund is an ongoing research fund and therefore additional research projects that have a specific focus on Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander health will be funded in the future.  IIMCHEY will cease funding 2027,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I342,Strengthening Digital Health Capacity,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Funding for the National Community Controlled Health Organisation state and territory Affiliate Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations for resourcing and services to assist Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Services to undertake activities to adopt and embed digital health in their respective regions to support their member services. Education and support activities for this FY include ongoing My Health Record sessions (Digital Health Yarning) for Affiliates; a series of My Health Record topics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health workers; MHR eLearning modules for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health workers (accredited by NAATSIHWP). The Agency is also working with the Affiliates to ensure culturally appropriate uploading of advance care information to MHR.,Resourcing: $1.65 million (inc GST) from 2022-2023 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by 30 June 2027,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I343,Supporting people with cancer Grant initiative,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Supporting people with cancer Grant initiative is an annual grants program that engages the community to improve outcomes for people affected by cancer. At least four grants per year are awarded, with at least two going to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Organisations.","Resourcing: 2020-21 $948,243 (ex GST), of which $470,270 (ex GST) was provided to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Organisations and 2021-22 $996,610 (GST excluding), of which $240,000 (GST excluding) was provided to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Organisations.
I344,Targeted research to support healthy ageing,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Six research grants into Healthy Ageing of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Five research grants into Dementia in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Both sets of grants were provided through NHMRC Targeted Calls for Research grant opportunities.,Resourcing: Healthy Ageing grants receive $6 million (over 5 years from 2019) and Dementia grants $14 million (over 5 years from 2018) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Healthy Ageing Grants (2023-2024) Dementia Grants (2022-2023),National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I345,Integrated Team Care (ITC) Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Funding to Primary Health Networks to manage the Integrated Team Care Program, which enables access to coordinated care for First Nations people with complex, multidisciplinary chronic care needs. The ITC program also seeks to improve access to culturally appropriate mainstream primary care services for First Nations people.",Resourcing: $221.1 million from 2021-22 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: This program is ongoing with current funding over four years 2021-22 to 2023-24.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I346,Puggy Hunter Memorial Scholarship Scheme (PHMSS),Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Puggy Hunter Memorial Scholarship Scheme (PHMSS) aims to increase the capacity of the First Nations health workforce through activities that promote and support Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples studying in health disciplines. Variation of the existing PHMSS In the 2022-23 Budget Measure, the Australian Government agreed to fund an additional $13.9 million for this scheme over 2 financial years from 2022-2023 to 2023-2024 to extend the program and to increase the number of scholarships by up to 150 scholarships per year.",Resourcing: $18.15 million from 2020-2021 to 2022-2023 and $13.9 million from 2022-2023 to 2023-24 (Variation)  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion by December 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I347,NHMRC Community Water Planner,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Health outcomes are improved by the provision of safe drinking water supplies to remote Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities through an updated, fit-for-purpose Community Water Planner. The NHMRC Community Water Planner is intended to support small water supplies around Australia such as those in remote Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities that are not serviced by larger water service providers. The tool was decommissioned by NHMRC in 2019 for IT security reasons and the need for a new tool or resources for co-design activities are currently being considered. scoped for redevelopment.","Resourcing: Funding to be sought from Commonwealth, jurisdictional and industry stakeholders for consultation, design and maintenance of any future tool. Scoping of this project is currently being undertaken under the current NHMRC budget allocation for the rolling revision of the Australian Drinking Water Guidelines. The recent 'Closing the Water for People and Communities Gap Improving water services to First Nations remote communities' report from the Water Services Association of Australia (launched by the Minister for Indigenous Australians in November 2022) recommended that $2 million be allocated to redevelop the NHMRC Community Water Planner. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: The timeframes for this project are currently being scoped and will be prioritised as part of the rolling revision of the Australian Drinking Water Guidelines. A consultation workshop was held in May 2023 at the national OzWater conference in Sydney.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,x,
I348,Birthing on Country Centre of Excellence,Stakeholder,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Construction of a Birthing on Country Centre of Excellence at Waminda, an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in Nowra, NSW. The Centre will support cultural safety during pregnancy, birth and the postnatal period. Birthing on Country models of care return maternity services to communities and First Nations control and enable self-determination and connection to family, culture and community across the perinatal period, which research highlights improves birth outcomes for First Nations mothers and babies.",Resourcing: $22.5 million over three financial years (2022-23 to 2024-25) Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Funding agreement has been offered and executed on 8 May 2023. Construction will commence in 2022-23 and the Birth Centre will be operational in 2025-26. A workforce transition plan will commence to ensure a ready maternal workforce when the Birth Centre opens.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I349,"Research that benefits Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander-led research",Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Since the program's inception in 2015, and as of 30 April 2023, the Medical Research Future Fund (MRFF) has invested $211.24 million in 93 grants with a focus on Indigenous health research. Of this amount, $67.69 million has been awarded through the MRFF's Indigenous Health Research Fund which has a focus on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander-led research. Under the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) Targeted Call for Research, four grants have been provided to research into nutrition in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People.  NHMRC continues to commit at least 5% of its funding of research, capacity building and translation to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health research and researchers. Each year NHMRC specifically conducts a TCR on a priority area of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health identified through a public consultation process. ",Resourcing: $211.24 million from the MRFF Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: The Medical Research Future Fund is an ongoing research fund and therefore additional research projects that have a specific focus on Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander health will be funded in the future.,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I350,Improving the health and wellbeing of First Nations mothers and babies,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Growing the maternity health workforce and redesigning maternity services to reach more First Nations pregnant women, inclusive of Birthing on Country and culturally safe continuous midwifery care activities. The measure will also continue and expand the delivery of the Australian Nurse-Family Partnership Program (ANFPP).","Resourcing: $32.2 million (2021-22 to 2024-25) for nine organisations to grow the maternity health workforce and redesign maternity services.  $12.8 million (2021-22 to 2024-25) to expand the ANFPP to two further sites in Western Australia (from 13 to 15 sites nationally) and $101.7 million over four years (2021-22 to 2024-25) to continue ANFPP delivery in the existing 13 sites. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: The Department is currently working with key stakeholders to determine how to support the funded Birthing on Country service models over the next three years and how to support the ANFPP expansion to WA including in the Kimberley and Pilbara region.  The Department is working in partnership with NACCHO to support delivery of these activities and support mapping, planning and coordination of maternal and child health activities more broadly. NACCHO are also undertaking a scoping exercise to inform the potential future development of a maternal and child health model of care. ",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I351,National Information and Awareness Campaign for Pregnant and Breastfeeding Women,Initiative,Campaign,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Campaign to raise awareness of the risks of drinking alcohol and awareness about Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorder (FASD) and funding for FASD diagnostic services to increase access and reduce waiting times.,Resourcing: The campaign receives $25 million (2019-20 to 2022 23) and an additional $2.4 million (2023-24) and $21 million for FASD diagnostic services Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Campaign completion by September 2024 and FASD diagnostic services funded to early-mid 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I352,"Closing the Gap Healthy Mums, Healthy Bubs measure",Initiative,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Implementation is underway of the Closing the Gap Healthy Mums, Healthy Bubs measure, through which the Commonwealth is investing $45 million over four years (2021-22 to 2024-25). This investment is being targeted towards:
I353,Connected Beginnings,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Health is working with NACCHO to expand the health component of the Connected Beginnings program to all existing and future sites funded by the Department of Education and to better support health services and integrate the health component of the program.,"Resourcing: $37.0 million (ex GST) from 2021-22 to 2024-25 (Department of Health and Aged Care). A further $4.7million is being provided from the Indigenous Australians’ Health Program to enable expansion of the health component to additional sites, bringing total investment from the Department  to $41.7 million.  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care|Assistant Minister for Indigenous Health Delivery Timeframe: The Department of Health and Aged Care will continue to expand the health component of Connected Beginnings to additional sites. Expansion to reach a minimum of 50 sites by 2024-25.",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I354,A study to understand the mental health needs of First Nations peoples,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"A study to understand the mental health needs of First Nations peoples, developed in partnership with First Nations peoples to ensure it is culturally appropriate and meets the needs of communities. The scope of this study is still being determined.",Resourcing: $30.5 million from 2021-22 to 2027-28 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion due by June 2028,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,x,,,,
I355,Head to Health Adult sites and national phone service,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Australian Government is expanding the Head to Health network of services to increase access to community mental health care that addresses a broad range of social, physical and emotional needs. An overarching principle of Head to Health services is to ensure cultural safety, in line with the Gayaa Dhuwi (Proud Spirit) Declaration and Priority Reform 3. Additionally, appropriate clinical governance, and safety and quality frameworks, informed by co-design and cultural safety considerations, are required to implement services. Head to Health adult sites provide a safe and welcoming place for adults to access mental health information, services and supports delivered by multidisciplinary care teams over extended hours, without needing a prior appointment or paying a fee. The Head to Health Phone Service operates nationally alongside physical Head to Health sites, offering a suite of entry points to access mental health advice, assessment and treatment. Anyone across Australia can call Head to Health on 1800 595 212 and speak with a mental health professional who will take the time to understand their individual circumstances and connect them to the best local mental health service to meet their clinical and cultural needs.","Resourcing: $487.2m over four years from 2021-22 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: There are currently nine Head to Health centres operating across the country, and 2 Head to Health satellites. A national network of 61 Head to Health adult sites is being established, with the final sites commencing establishment in 2025-26. Timing varies across states and territories. The National Head to Health Phone Service commenced operation on 1 July 2022.",National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,x,,,,
I356,Head to Health Kids Hubs,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is working in partnership with states and territories to establish a national network of Head to Health Kids Hubs (mental health and wellbeing centres) for children aged 0 - 12 years and their families. Through an integrated, multidisciplinary team approach the Kids Hubs will deliver a range of specialist medical and allied health services (including culturally appropriate services), enabling families to access a range of supports and services including for behavioural and developmental concerns. The Kids Hubs will complement and integrate with existing state funded maternal and child health services, including child and adolescent mental health services, to ensure seamless support and transition for families and to make a complex system more accessible.",Resourcing: $54.2 million from 2021-22 to 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Varies across states and territories with 12 Kids Hubs to be established in the 2022-23 with a national network of 17 established by 30 June 2026,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,x,,,,
I358,Increasing the research and evidence-base,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Five research grants have been funded through an NHMRC Targeted Call for Research into Social and Emotional Wellbeing and Mental Health for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples from Early Life to Young Adults.,Resourcing: $6 million over 5 years from 2018 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2023-2024,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I359,Mental health workforce,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Boost and strengthen the mental health workforce to ensure equitable access to services. The National Mental Health Workforce Strategy (Strategy) has been endorsed by Health Ministers and is expected to be published in early 2023. First Nations mental health and suicide prevention workers are identified as a priority profession under the National Mental Health and Suicide Prevention Agreement. A number of programs, informed by the Strategy, are being implemented to boost the mental health workforce.","Resourcing: * $8.3 million to support greater representation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in the mental health workforce and Mental Health First Aid training 
I360,National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Plan - Mental Health,Commitment,Action Plan,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"All 31 PHNs are funded to lead primary mental health and suicide prevention planning and service commissioning at a regional level. This includes funding for culturally appropriate, evidence-based metal health services specific to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people","Resourcing: For the period of 2016-17 to 2023-24 a total of $238.4 million has been allocated to this activity. In the 2021-22 Budget, $1.5 million has been committed to undertake a review of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health sector (Capability Review) delivering mental health services for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24",National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,x,,,,
I361,National Mental Health and Suicide Prevention Funding,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"To address the high rates of suicide and mental-ill health of First Nations people, communities and families, the Australian Government is investing in a range of First Nations mental health and suicide prevention initiatives. This includes funding for:
I362,Targeted Regional Initiatives for Suicide Prevention,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The 2022-23 Budget included an investment of $42.3 million in targeted regional initiatives for suicide prevention (2022-23 to 2023-24). This builds on lessons from the National Suicide Prevention Trial across 12 Primary Health Network (PHN) regions and expands activities across 31 PHNs nationally. This measure aims to support the transition and learnings from the National Suicide Prevention Trial environment towards building the capacity of all PHNs to implement evidence based, systems-oriented, and community led approaches to suicide prevention across Australia. Funding will support a dedicated Suicide Prevention Regional Response Coordinator in each PHN as well as additional investment in regional and community based suicide prevention interventions. PHNs may commission Indigenous specific projects under this imitative. The Suicide Prevention Regional Response Coordinator will also be a key contact for the National Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation (NACCHO) Culture Care Connect Program.",Resourcing: $42.3 million from 2022-23 to 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2024,National,N/A,,,,x,x,x,,,,x,,,,
I363,Improving Cultural Safety across the health system through the Medical Research Future Fund Indigenous Health Research Grant Opportunity,Initiative,Research,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Medical Research Future Fund's (MRFF) Indigenous Health Research Fund has awarded the following research grants related to improving cultural safety: - $3 million has been provided for four research projects on improving Cultural Safety across the health system through the MRFF 2021 Indigenous Health Research Grant Opportunity. - $1.4 million has been provided under the 2020 MRFF Indigenous Health Research Grant Opportunity to the University of Sydney to develop a patient reported experience measure for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people accessing primary health care. -$0.99 million has been provided under the 2021 Consumer-Led research grant opportunity to foster culturally respectful and safe care for Aboriginal people in dementia care. -$5 million has been provided by NHMRC for a Targeted Call for Research on the commercial determinants of Indigenous health, this will build on research on the social and cultural determinants of health.","Resourcing: $5.39 million  $5 million NHMRC Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: Projects funded through the 2021 Indigenous Health Grant Opportunity are expected to be completed on 31 May 2024.
I364,Powering Australia Industry Growth Centre - First Nations Business Advisor,Initiative,Capacity Building,"Department of Industry, Science and Resources ",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The Powering Australia Industry Growth Centre (PAIGC) will provide advanced technology and skills to businesses looking to locally manufacture renewable energy technologies. The PAIGC will be established by competitive grant process. This PAIGC will support First Nations businesses to contribute to the development of renewable technologies through engagement of a First Nations Business Advisor. Further details will be confirmed through the grant process.,Resourcing: Up to $14 million available for the Growth Centre grant.   Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister Husic  Delivery Timeframe: Funding provided up until 2026-27.,National,N/A,,,,x,,x,,,,,,,,
I365,Early Childhood Outreach Initiative,Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"In August 2022, the Minister for the NDIS announced funding for the Early Childhood Outreach Initiative (Outreach). Outreach will support young First Nations children aged 0-8 years with newly identified disability or emerging developmental concerns and their families, parents, carers and communities through targeted and culturally sensitive activities. Further consultation with key stakeholders will inform the design and activities under Outreach. ","Resourcing: $15 million from 2022-23 to 2025-26, funded under the Information, Linkages and Capacity Building Program Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the National Disability Insurance Scheme Delivery Timeframe: Some services under Outreach are expected to commence mid 2023, with consultation expected mid-late 2023. ",National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I366,Improving support for people with disability - Increasing the capacity of the supported employment sector to support people with disability with high support needs,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Commonwealth is investing $57 million over four years to:  * create ongoing employment opportunities for people with disability with high support needs; * assist the supported employment sector to evolve to better meet community expectations; and * provide people with disability with high support needs, their families and carers with access to advocacy support and information to build their confidence and understanding about rights and options at work. The department  will be consulting with the sector, including with First Nation's people with disability, on the design of the initiatives to ensure supports are appropriately targeted,.  ",Resourcing: $57 million Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: 2023-2027,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I368,Increase Indigenous cultural capability ,Initiative,Capacity Building,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Increase Indigenous cultural capability of the agency through targeted initiatives: • mandate tier 1 cultural e-learning for all staff. • investigate and identify critical roles/teams where tier 2 facilitated learning is required. • all SES to participate in facilitated learning within 12 months. • anti-racism working group to implement learning products, tools and processes.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services  Delivery Timeframe: ,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I369,Indigenous Service Design Standards,Commitment,Other,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Re-imagine Services Australia’s Indigenous Service Design Standards in line with the new approach of the National Agreement. ,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services  Delivery Timeframe: ,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I370,National Indigenous Coalition ,Stakeholder,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,Services Australia ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Re-imagine Services Australia’s National Indigenous Coalition (the agency’s peak Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander body) as the agency’s new Indigenous Voice in line with the new approach of the National Agreement. ,Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Government Services  Delivery Timeframe: By 1/09/2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I371,Remote Air Services Subsidy (RASS) Scheme,Initiative,Funding,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Provide regular air services to (currently) 86 designated remote communities to facilitate delivery of essential supplies, and passenger services to enable residents to attend appointments in regional centres. ","Resourcing: $64.4 million over the four years from 2023-24 to 2026-27. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development and Local Government/ Assistant Minister for Regional Development Delivery Timeframe: Ongoing",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,x,,,,
I372,Remote Airstrip Upgrade Program,Initiative,Program,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,The program provides grant funding for critical airstrip upgrades to improve aviation safety and support ongoing air access to remote communities.,"Resourcing: $12 million in the 2023 Budget for Round 10 of the program Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development and Local Government/ Assistant Minister for Regional Development Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2026",National,N/A,,,,,,,,,x,x,,,,
I373,Revive - Creative Australia - First Nations-led body,Stakeholder,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,"Department of Infrastructure, Transport, Regional Development, Communications and the Arts",Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Revive aims to advance First Nations agency, financial and creative control and provide opportunities for First Nations work – both traditional and contemporary – across all art forms. The establishment of a First Nations-led body within Creative Australia is critical to these ambitions. The body will be established following an extensive consultation process and its functions will consider the needs of the sector. The body will identify and plan for strategic investments, commencing activities in 2024. First Nations arts and cultural initiatives will also continue to be funded through existing programs within Creative Australia.",Resourcing: $35.5 million from 2024-25 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for the Arts Delivery Timeframe: 2024-25,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,x,,,,,
I374,Digitisation of at-risk audio and video collections held by First Nations broadcasters and community organisations,Initiative,Program,Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Community-led preservation and digitisation activities will be facilitated by AIATSIS and First Nations Media Australia (FNMA), the First Nations broadcasting and media sector peak body.   The AIATSIS Central Australia digitisation facility in Alice Springs opened in  2023 and will act as a hub for the project, with mobile digitisation kits and data storage enabling on country training, digitisation and storage of audio and visual materials.  This will enable digitisation to happen on or as close to Country as possible. Once digitised the material remains the property of the community-based organisation, however they will be encouraged to consider making the digitised content available for inclusion in the AIATSIS collection, and/or made publicly accessible through Trove or IndigiTube.",Resourcing: $3.8 million over two years from 1 July 2023 is being provided in the 2023-24 Budget Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians  Delivery Timeframe: By 30 June 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,x,,,,x,x
I375,Central Australia Response - On-Country Learning,Initiative,Program,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,$40 million in additional funding to help schools in Central Australia better engage with young people and increase school enrolment and education outcomes.,"Resourcing: $40 million over the 2023 and 2024 calendar years. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education |  Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Bilateral agreement to be negotiated with the NT Government in mid-2023, with implementation following shortly after, and the measure ceasing on 31 December 2024",National,N/A,,,,x,,,,x,,,,,,
I376,Clontarf ,Stakeholder,NGO,Department of Education (Commonwealth),Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Clontarf Foundation supports up to 12,500 First Nations boys and young men to improve their confidence, school engagement, Year 12 attainment and post school pathways. ",Resourcing: $65 million over 2023 and 2024 calendar years. Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Education  Delivery Timeframe: 1 April 2023 - 31 December 2023,National,N/A,,,,,,,,x,,,,,,
I377,National Redress Scheme- Redress Support Services,Initiative,Program,Department of Social Services,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The National Redress Scheme funds Redress Support Services to provide practical and emotional support for survivors of institutional child sexual abuse who want to engage with the Scheme. This includes Indigenous-specific Redress Support Services operating across Australia delivering free, specialist services to First Nations peoples who need support to engage with the Scheme. ",Resourcing: Twelve Indigenous-specific Redress Support Services are funded a total of $7.9 million (GST exclusive) over two years to 2023-24. Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Social Services Delivery Timeframe: Services are currently funded to 30 June 2024,National,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,,,,
I379,Indigenous aged care  assessment organisations,Stakeholder,Other,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"Budget 2023-2024 re-iterated 2021-22 Budget commitment to establish First Nations aged care assessment organisations as part of the new single assessment workforce, to simplify and provide access aged care.   Further engagements with First Nations service providers are expected leading up to the implementation date.  Feedback has and will continue to  inform the design and  the approach to establishing the workforce.  ",Resourcing: $4.2 million in 2024-2025 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2025,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I380,"Boost mental health support for First Nations peoples in the lead up to, during and following the referendum to enshrine an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Voice in the Constitution.",Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"As part of the 2023-24 Budget, the Australian Government announced $10.5 million in 2023-24 to boost mental health support for First Nations peoples in the lead up to, during and following the referendum to enshrine an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Voice in the Constitution.","Resourcing: In the 2023-24 Budget: - $10m to NACCHO in 2023-24 to increase capacity of ACCHOs to provide culturally-appropriate mental health services for First Nations peoples, allowing flexibility to target additional support when and where it is required; and - $0.5m in 2023-24 to monitor and report the experiences and mental health and wellbeing needs of First Nations peoples in the lead up to and immediately after the referendum, to help inform targeted responses to combat racism and its impacts, and more effectively direct supports to First Nations peoples.  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24",National,N/A,,,,,x,,x,,,x,,,,
I381,Interim First Nations Aged Care Commissioner,Initiative,Reform,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The 2023-24 Budget is providing $1.6m to stand-up an interim First Nations Aged Care Commissioner for 12 months to lead a consultation process with First Nations communities and stakeholders on the design and functions of a permanent Commissioner role, including where it is located. These consultations will form the basis for a proposal to Government on the permanent Commissioner in 2024. The interim Commissioner will also provide a voice for First Nations people and providers that will contribute to the ongoing reforms to the aged care system.  ",Resourcing: $1.6m in 2023-24 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister Wells Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2024,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,
I382,Integrated Care & Commissioning Project,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care ,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The 2023-24 Budget is providing $27 million over four years to improve the provision and access to care and support services in thin markets by trialling integrated services and joint commissioning across primary health, First Nations health services, disability, aged care, and veterans' care services in up to 10 locations across the country.",Resourcing: $27m from 2023-24 to 2027-28 Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s:  Delivery Timeframe: By 1/06/2028,National,N/A,,,,x,x,x,x,,,x,,,,
I383,"Cultural safety, trauma aware, and healing informed care principles and training for aged care staff ",Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,Government is investing $5.3 million over 4 years to embed  cultural safety trauma-aware and healing-informed care principles and training across all aged care services to improve access to culturally safe care for First Nations elders. To be delivered across all aged care services to improve access to culturally safe care for First Nations Elders.,In development. Resourcing: $5.3 million from 2023-24 to 2027-28 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s:  Delivery Timeframe: Training in place Jul 24,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I384,Regional Insights for Indigenous Communities (RIFIC) website,Initiative,Resource,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The Department of Health has funded the AIHW to develop the Regional Insights for Indigenous Communities (RIFIC) website that brings together a range of government data at the local level into the one location. RIFIC has been designed to be simple, easy to use, a flexible platform that can be used to present statistics or analytical products with a geographic component. The AIHW has also developed a communication strategy to ensure the website is publicised and utilised by a wide range of stakeholders. The website was launched on 13 December 2021. There will be continuing updates to the website during this time, with major releases of upgrades and updates annually. The first update was released on 9 December 2022.",Resourcing: Funded by the Department of Health and Aged Care Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2024-25,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I385,Community Data Project,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The National Indigenous Australians Agency has funded the AIHW to develop secure community data portals, for up to six communities. The project will enable participating communities and organisations to obtain access to and have the capability to use locally relevant data and information to set and monitor the implementation of efforts to Close the Gap (CTG) and monitor progress towards their priorities.",Resourcing: Funded by the National Indigenous Australians Agency Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2021-22 to 2023-24,National,N/A,,,,,,,x,,,,,,,
I386,Improving First Nations Cancer Outcomes (2023-24 Budget measure) ,Initiative,Target,Cancer Australia,Other,x,,,,,Cancer Australia to ensure mainstream cancer care services are culturally safe and accessible for First Nations people.  Initiatives include: to ensure mainstream cancer care services are culturally safe and accessible for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.  to strengthen Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cancer research and scholarships.,Resourcing: Cancer Australia funding- $38.6m over 4 years from 2023-24  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24 - 2026-27,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
I387,Strengthening Indigenous research capacity and capability ,Initiative,Capacity Building,NHMRC ,Other,x,,,,,NHMRC National Network for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Researchers -is a single collaborative network  of researchers and their supports  who have the aim of building capacity and capability of current and future indigenous researchers ,Resourcing: $10 million over 5 years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2021-2025,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I388,Improving First Nations Cancer outcomes,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care| Cancer Australia,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"This initiative will provide funding to the National Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Organisation (NACCHO), and the Aboriginal Community-Controlled Health Services (ACCHS) sector to respond to and support cancer care needs on the ground, tailored to local need and priorities (consistent with Priority Reform Two). The Program will include employment of up to 260 new positions including 100 Health Promotion Officers, 100 Cancer Support Officers, 10 Cancer Liaison Officers and 50 Cancer Treatment clinicians.   It will also ensure mainstream cancer care services are culturally safe and accessible to First Nations people and that there is coordinated delivery of cancer specialist and primary health care to an optimal standard, in partnership with the ACCHS sector (consistent with Priority Reform Three).",Resourcing: $238.5m over four years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care | Assistant Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: Completion by 30 June 2027,National,N/A,,,,,x,x,,,,x,,,,
I389,National Lung Cancer Screening Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care| Cancer Australia,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"National Lung Cancer Screening Program As announced in the 2023-24 Budget, the Government has committed $263.8 million over four years, ongoing for the establishment of a new National Lung Cancer Screening Program, including $23.1 million to enable partnership with NACCHO and  support for ACCHO partnerships and  priority population groups.  Funding has been included for mobile screening and patient assisted transport services to ensure equitable access, particularly for people in the most vulnerable population groups.   Partnership with NACCHO will ensure the program is culturally safe and provides equitable access. Governance for the program is currently under consideration as part of program implementation, and will reflect the need to partner with NACCHO on the successful delivery of the program. ",Resourcing: 263.8 m over four years  Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: To be launched in July 2025,National,N/A,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
I390,Support ACCOs capacity to deliver aged care services,Initiative,Capacity Building,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,$8.2 million over two years to support up to 10 Aboriginal Community Controlled Organisations (ACCOs) to develop capacity and capability to deliver aged care services to First Nations elders. Organisations skilled in developing organisation capacity and capability will be engaged to establish aged care clinical and financial management systems and develop workforce capacity.  Organisations will work with ACCOs over 2 years to support them to deliver aged care services in their community ,Resourcing: $8.2 million over two years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2025,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I391,Increased funding for very remote NATSIFAC Program Residential Services,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,$52.1 million over two years to support very remote NATSIFAC residential providers . The additional funding will ease the financial pressure on NATSIFAC residential providers operating in very remote or isolated communities and recognises the higher costs of delivering services in these locations.,Resourcing: $52.1 million over two years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2025,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I392,Support NATSIFAC Program services to meet regulations under the new Aged Care Act,Initiative,Funding,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,$8.1 million over three years to support NATSIFAC Providers to meet regulations under the new Aged Care Act. This tailored support will enhance NATSIFAC providers capacity and capability to comply with all new obligations. ,Resourcing: $8.1 million over two years Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2026,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I393,Boost to funding for First Nations Aged care Programs ,Initiative,Program,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,$58.9 million commencing 30 June 2023 to support outcome of the Fair Work Commission case. This support will boost funding for First Nations Aged Care Programs and Multi-Purpose Services ,Resourcing: $58.9 million commencing 30 June 2023 Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: By 1/07/2024,National,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,
I394,Increase uptake of Indigenous-specific health assessments and a national rollout of the  Institute of Urban Indigenous Health’s Deadly Choices program,Initiative,Target,Department of Health and Aged Care,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"The 2023-24 Budget announced $11.5M over three years to promote uptake of health assessments. This includes: - $3.5 million in 2023-24 to develop, in partnership with ACCHSs and mainstream health sectors, a national campaign to raise awareness of health assessments and expand the reach of the Deadly Choices initiative.  - $8 million over two years from 2024-25 for improved administration and coordination of health assessments and their linkages with allied health services.  ",Resourcing: $11.5 M Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care Delivery Timeframe: 2023-24 to 2024-25,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,x,,,,
I395,Commonwealth Prioritisation Policy Guidance,Initiative,Other,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,"NIAA will create a suite of materials to guide and support all Commonwealth agencies to develop and implement prioritisation policies within their existing grant programs and procurement activities. This is to be done in partnership with their stakeholders. As part of these materials, the NIAA will consider including supporting guidance on cultural safety for organisations contracted by government to deliver services to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.",Resourcing: Costs are to be absorbed within existing resources Indigenous Specific: No Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: 2023,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I396,Building the community controlled sector joint virtual funding pool - Addressing workforce barriers for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workers and Practitioners ,Initiative,Funding,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,x,,,,,This project provides funding to the National Association of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workers and Practitioners (NAATSIHWP) to  consult across all jurisdictions to identify barriers to expansion and professional utilisation of the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health Workers and Practitioners workforce and provide next steps to address these barriers.  This is funding under the Building the Community-Controlled Sector Joint Virtual Funding Pool.,Resourcing: $0.995 million  Indigenous Specific: Yes Responsible Minister/s: Minister for Health and Aged Care | Minister for Indigenous Australians Delivery Timeframe: By 30/06/2032,National,N/A,,,,,x,,,,,,,,,
I397,Cape York Peninsula Tenure Resolution Program,Initiative,Program,Department of Environment and Science,State Government,,,,,,Returns ownership and management of identified lands on Cape York Peninsula to local Aboriginal Traditional Owners.,,State,Queensland,N/A,N/A,N/A,,x,,,,,,,,,x
I398,Engaging Indigenous Australians in STEM,Initiative,Program,"Department of Industry, Science and Resources ",Commonwealth Government,,,,,,The Government is investing in a range of STEM engagement programs and initiatives that are supporting Indigenous Australians to participate in STEM,,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,,,x,,x,x,,,,,
I399,Industry Growth Program,Initiative,Program,"Department of Industry, Science and Resources ",Commonwealth Government,,,,,,"The Industry Growth Program aims to support Australian industry to become more innovative, productive and competitive and capture opportunities from a growing global economy. The Industry Growth Division and AusIndustry will provide advice and matched funding for SMEs and startups to commercialise their ideas and grow their business.  ",,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,,x,,,,x,,,,,
I400,NationalMap ,Initiative,Resource,Geoscience Australia,Commonwealth Government,,,,,,NationalMap is an online map-based tool that allows easy access to location-based data from Australian government agencies. Geoscience Australia is working with Cooperative Research Centre for Developing Northern Australia (CRCNA) to integrate traditional knowledge and information into Geographic Information System (GIS) technology in culturally appropriate and inclusive ways.,,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,,,,x,,,,,,,
I401,Collaborative Research on First Foods,Initiative,Research,"National Measurement Institute; Department of Industry, Science and Resources",Commonwealth Government,,,,,,"The National Measurement Institute (NMI) is undertaking a collaborative research project on First Foods with RMIT university. The researcher is the Chancellor’s Indigenous - Pre-Doctoral research fellow undertaking the characterization of a range of First Foods and comparing them with counterpart western foods as well as attempting to discover novel compounds for their potential health benefits, allergenic properties and toxicity using LC-QTOF-MS. The projects are designed as such that the IP of the research outcomes are for the indigenous landholders. The research conducted will be presented at numerous forums including those organised by NMI.",,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,x,,,x,,x,,,,,x
I402,Indigenous Knowledge Project,Initiative,Reform,IP Australia,Commonwealth Government,,,,"IP Australia is also leading a cross-government group, which includes the NIAA, to assess the scope and feasibility of stand-alone legislation to protect IK.",,IP Australia’s Indigenous Knowledge (IK) Project is looking at a range of initiatives so the Intellectual Property (IP) system can better support Traditional Owners to use and protect their IK.  ,,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,x,,,x,,,,,,,
