N078,The ACT's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Elected Body (ATSIEB),Network,Advisory Body,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,,x,,,,"The ACT Government benefits from 15 years working in formal partnership with the democratic Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Elected Body (Elected Body). The Elected Body was established in 2008 to enable the full diversity of the ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community to have a strong, democratically elected voice to government. This was done in partnership with a previous ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Consultative Council, which provided advice to and worked in partnership with the ACT Government. The Elected Body, representing the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community in the ACT, is an equal partner to government in the development of the ACT Agreement. Through the ACT Agreement, the ACT Government and the Elected Body are committed to supporting the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community to actively influence and participate in social, economic and cultural life. The Elected Body has the power to hold public hearings, providing opportunity to hold Ministers and officials to account and giving the Elected Body the access it requires to appropriately scrutinise the Governments implementation of the ACT Agreement. The Elected Body are represented on both the ACT Government’s Strategic Board (as active participants) and Inter-Directorate (as co-chair) Committees, consulting and advising on a broad range of government policy and program delivery. These Committees are whole-of-government forums and are inclusive of ACT Government Senior Executive and officials with oversight and responsibility for providing strategic leadership on all aspects of the ACT and National agreements. The Elected Body is also a member of the national Coalition of Peaks, and together with the ACT Government, as members of the Joint Council, work to implement the National Agreement.",Example of a local and regional Voice model in the ACT,Territory,ACT,,,,x,,x,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N079,Dhawura Ngunnawal Caring for Country Committee,Network,Committee,Environment Planning and Sustainable Development Directorate (EPSDD),Territory Government,,,,,,"The Dhawura Ngunnawal Caring for Country Committee (DNCCC) is a partnership mechanism that facilitates the active engagement of Traditional Custodians in the work of the Environment, Planning and Sustainable Development Directorate (EPSDD). The DNCCC empowers self-determination for Ngunnawal people, ensuring they can speak for their Country and that Ngunnawal cultural values, knowledge and aspirations have a distinct role in the natural and built environments of the ACT. The committee meet monthly and provide advice, guidance and decision-making for several activities including but not limited to the following: Cultural Resource Management Plan, Ngunnawal Ngadjung Water Initiative, ACT Water Governance, Mulligan's Flat Woodland Sanctuary Governance Reform and Implementation Plan, Tidbinbilla Functional Review and the National Koala Monitoring Program. The Dhawura Ngunnawal Committee will engage, connect and work with the Environment Planning and Sustainable Development Directorate (EPSDD) to identify and implement cultural understanding within the Directorate and to provide guidance, direction and decisions on environmental and land management matters including land, fire, air and water to better manage Ngunnawal Country together on EPSDD managed lands.  This partnership improves engagement with Ngunnawal people to work together and support the integration of cultural knowledge in management of Country and to provide support and assist in a decision-making capacity for the Directorate and its staff, Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and non-Aboriginal.",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,x,x,,,,,,,,,x
N080,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Consumer Reference Group,Network,Reference Group,Canberra Health Services,Territory Government,,,,,,"An Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Consumer Reference Group (within the Canberra Health Services), with people from ACT and NSW to share their experiences, provide cultural advice and direction, and build a strong partnership. They work to ensure the community has input into services provided by CHS and information provided to the community.",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,x,,,,,,x,,,,x
N081,"Our Booris, Our Way Implementation Oversight Committee",Network,Committee,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,,,,,,"Established in June 2020 to monitor implementation and provide a cultural lens to guide the systemic reform necessary of the ACT's child protection system to progress in partnership with the ACT Government the recommendations set out in the 2019 Our Booris, Our Way review. The Oversight Committee meets monthly to consider various elements of the work, with detailed quarterly updates provided by relevant Directorates. Following the review, an Our Booris, Our Way Implementation Oversight Committee (OBOW IOC) was established as an exclusively Aboriginal committee who continue to oversee the implementation of the 28 recommendations and 8 sub ACT Government 5 ACT Annual Report recommendations of the review, ensuring that self-determination remains central to how the Government responds to the significant challenge of overrepresentation in child protection",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,x,,x,,,,x,,x,,x
N082,Strategic Board Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs Committee,Network,Committee,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,,x,,,,"The Strategic Board Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs Committee provides strategic leadership and oversight across the ACT Public Service driving policy, program, and service delivery reform to enable self-determination for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities in the ACT. Strategic Indicators identified in the ACT ATSI Agreement 2019-2028 will be reported to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Sub Committee of the ACT Strategic Board and made publicly available via an annual statement of performance by the Minister for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs.",P.g. 14 of ACT CTG implementation plan 2022,Territory,ACT,,,,,,x,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N083,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs Inter-Directorate Committee (IDC) ,Network,Committee,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,x,x,,,,"The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs Inter-Directorate Committee (IDC) supports the Strategic Board Committee and drives the implementation of the ACT and National Agreements. In 2020, the IDC introduced Co-Chairing arrangements between the Elected Body and the ACT Government to acknowledge the importance of shared decision making and the strong voice of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,x,,x,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N084,Upper Murrumbidgee Catchment Network (UMCN),Network,Network,N/A,NGO,,,x,,,"The Upper Murrumbidgee Catchment Network (UMCN) is a key organisation for networking, learning and discussing natural resource management issues and activities across the Upper Murrumbidgee Catchment. The UMCN provides a strong connection between community groups and government bodies and brings people together across jurisdictional boundaries in clear recognition that NRM issues do not stop at state or council boundaries",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,x,,,,,,,,,,x
N085,ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Public Service Network (Murranga Murranga),Network,Network,Office for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs,Territory Government,,x,,,"The Network has an executive team consisting of 2 co-chairs and 2 team members. The Network also consists of four working groups: employment, mentoring and leadership, workforce retention, cultural integrity","The ACT Public Service Indigenous Network was established in January 2008 and provides an opportunity for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people employed in the ACT Public Service to contribute to improving program and service delivery by the ACT Government. The network is known as Murranga Murranga, a Ngunnawal word given to the network by the United Ngunnawal Elders Council. Murranga by itself means ‘hand’ and together Murranga Murranga means ‘hand in hand’.",Secretariat and adminstrative supprt for Murranga Murranga is provided by the Office for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Affairs,Territory,ACT,,,,,,x,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N086,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Eminent Panel for Community Engagement and Healing,Network,Advisory Body,Community Services Directorate,Territory Government,,x,,,,"The ACT Government will establish an Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Eminent Panel for Community Engagement and Healing to focus on healing, truth telling, advice to government on cultural recognition of First Peoples in the ACT and surrounding regions, and broader engagement with the diverse local Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community. The Eminent Panel will work with community to develop recommendations to the ACT Government. Acknowledging the need for community to lead this work, facilitated engagement will commence in 2023 supported by The Healing Foundation, a national Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander organisation that provides a platform to amplify the voices and lived experience of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. The community consultation to be undertaken by the Eminent Panel will be broad and will likely include recommendations for how to re-configure representation, governance and engagement mechanisms. This could include recommendations to redesign existing representative and advisory bodies, noting that the terms of reference for the Eminent Panel have not been determined at this stage. The Eminent Panel itself, as a body, will not replace any existing representative or advisory bodies.",The Eminent Panel is expected to conclude its consultations and make recommendations to Government in late 2024. This timeframe could change depending on when the Eminent Panel appointments take place.,Territory,ACT,,,,,x,x,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N087,ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Mental Health and Suicide Prevention Partnership Group,Network,Partnership Group,ACT Health Directorate,Territory Government,,,,,,"The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Mental Health and Suicide Prevention Partnership Group has operated since July 2019, and is chaired by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representatives and attended by ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander independent community members and representatives from ACT Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Government and NGOs. This group provides advice on culturally appropriate Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander suicide prevention and mental health strategies, as well guiding the implementation of these strategies. The group reports to the Coordinator General Mental Health and Wellbeing, Office for Mental Health, and Wellbeing.",n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,,,x,,,,x,,x,,x
N088,Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Procurement Policy Committee (ATSIPPC) ,Network,Committee,Procurement ACT (TendersACT),Territory Government,,,,,,The ATSIPPC will take a leading role in reviewing the ACT's ATSIPP and will reconvene as necessary to deliver the one year and three year review of the policy.,n.a.,Territory,ACT,,,,,x,x,,,x,,,,,x
N089,Aboriginal Outcomes Task Force,Network,Taskforce,Department of Communities & Justice,State Government,,,,,,"In response to the Family is Culture report, the NSW Government established an Aboriginal Outcomes Taskforce to drive improvements in services and supports for Aboriginal families, data collection and reporting, casework policy and practice, and interagency coordination.",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,,,,x,,x,,x,x
N090,NSW Aboriginal Education Consultative Group (NSW AECG),Network,Consultative Group,N/A,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,,,,,,"The NSW Aboriginal Education Consultative Group Inc. is a non for profit Aboriginal organisation that provides advice on all matters relevant to education and training with the mandate that this advice represents the Aboriginal community viewpoint. Our strong member base is key to the organisation being the peak advisory body regarding Aboriginal Education and Training at both State and Commonwealth levels. As a wholly volunteer based organisation, our key focus is ‘community first’; the educational rights of Aboriginal people in NSW being our driving force. The NSW AECG Inc. has local, regional and state network that enables effective communication allowing Aboriginal community viewpoint to be echoed throughout the organisation.",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,x,,,x,,,,,
N091,NSW Aboriginal Tourism Operators Council (NATOC),Network,Advisory Body,N/A,Peak Body,,,,,,Destination NSW will work closely with NATOC and the broader Aboriginal community to ensure all marketing and development activity is delivered in a culturally  acceptable manner. The main vision of NATOC is to give voice to Aboriginal owned tourism expertise to better inform industry and provide mentoring to Aboriginal tourism entrepreneurs to fulfil their business journeys.,n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,,,x,,x,,,,
N092,NSW Aboriginal Business Roundtable,Network,Reference Group,N/A,Peak Body,,,,,,"The convening of Aboriginal business roundtables to hear directly from Aboriginal businesses on what challenges they face to enter, grow and thrive in the NSW business sector. It is a significant event aimed at fostering connections between regional Aboriginal businesses, local councils, and government stakeholders.",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,,,x,,x,,,,
N093,Aboriginal Business Taskforce,Network,Taskforce,Aboriginal Affairs NSW (AANSW),State Government,,,,,,"The Taskforce will report directly to the Minister for Aboriginal Affairs and Treaty and will provide independent advice, develop strategic approaches and coordinate and drive initiatives under Priority Reform 5 relating to starting, supporting and growing businesses. ",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,,,x,,x,,,,
N094,NSW Joint Council,Network,Joint Council,Aboriginal Affairs NSW (AANSW),State Government,x,x,,NSW PWG,"All NSW CAPO members; Secretary, NSW Premier's Department","The NSW Government and NSW CAPO, as representatives of Aboriginal communities, have agreed to joint governance arrangements to develop, implement and monitor NSW’s plan to Closing the Gap. These joint governance arrangements make up the NSW Partnership for Closing the Gap. The NSW Joint Council (NSWJC) is the key decision-making group overseeing the planning and implementation of the National Agreement on Closing the Gap in NSW. It is jointly chaired by the NSW Minister for Aboriginal Affairs, the Hon. David Harris and the chair of NSW CAPO, Councillor Anne Dennis, NSWALC, and John Leha, AbSec.",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x,x
N095,NSW Partnership Working Group,Network,Working Group,New South Wales Government,State Government,x,,,,,"The NSW Partnership Working Group is made up of NSW Government senior executives across relevant departments, NSW CAPO members, and representatives from the National Indigenous Australians Agency, Local Government NSW, and the NSW Coalition of Aboriginal Regional Alliances. The NSW PWG is co-chaired by the Deputy Secretary, Community Engagement in the Department of Premier and Cabinet, and the Head of the NSW CAPO Secretariat, CEO of the NSW Aboriginal Land Council. Membership comprises NSW CAPO member organisations and affiliate members, senior-level representation from each NSW Government agency, NSW local government representation, NSW Coalition of Aboriginal Regional Alliances, the National Indigenous Australians Agency and other key Aboriginal interest groups as required. The NSW PWG supports the NSW Joint Council and is responsible for monitoring performance and delivery, and endorsing key deliverables and processes to develop the plan, while considering stakeholder needs.","NIAA has an observer role on the NSW PWG, and we are working to redefine NIAA’s role from observer to a more active role.",State,NSW,,,,x,,x,,,x,x,x,x,x,x
N096,NSW Officer-Level Working Groups,Network,Working Group,New South Wales Government,State Government,x,,,,,"The Officer-Level Working Groups are co-chaired by NSW CAPO and NSW Government cluster leads. Membership comprises NSW CAPO, NSW Government lead cluster representatives, NSW Coalition of Aboriginal Regional Alliances, other Aboriginal interest groups, Local Government NSW and other NSW government officials as determined. The working groups provide subject matter expertise to develop, progress and deliver action towards meeting the Priority Reforms and 17 Socio-Economic Outcomes in NSW.",n.a.,State,NSW,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
N097,NSW Partnership for Closing the Gap Agreement,Network,Partnership,New South Wales Government,State Government,x,,,,"NSW State government, NSW CAPO","The NSW Government and NSW CAPO, as representatives of Aboriginal communities, have agreed to joint governance arrangements to develop, implement and monitor NSW's plan to Closing the Gap. These joint governance arrangements make up the NSW Partnership for Closing the Gap.",n.a. ,State,NSW,,,,x,,,,,x,x,x,x,x,x
N098,Regional Aboriginal Water Committees ,Network,Committee,Department of Planning and Environment NSW,State Government,,,,,16 committees,"The purpose of the committees is to give greater recognition to Aboriginal water rights and interests, ensuring Aboriginal people can contribute to water management. 14 committees have been established across North, West and South NSW. Committee members will: empower Aboriginal communities to participate in water programs, build new relationships across warer, embed Aboriginal rights & values in water planning programs and projects, facilitate water knowledge exchanges in regional communities, assist government to identify key stakeholders in Aboriginal community for engagement, share own knowledge, and work in partnership with the department and other water agencies to contribute to water policy review and development. ",n.a. ,Regional,NSW,,"Border Rivers, Far North Coast, Gwydir, Hunter, Namoi, North Coast | Macquarie/Castlereagh, North-western, South-western | Lachlan, Murray, Murrumbidgee, South Coast, and Sydney",,x,,,,,,,,,,
N099,Aboriginal Cultural Heritage Advisory Committee,Network,Committee,Department of Planning and Environment NSW,State Government,,,,,"13 members in total, with 1 member nominated by the NSW CAPO, 1 member nominated by the Heritage Council of New South Wales, 1 member nominated by the NTSCORP Limited, and the remaining 10 members appointed from either Aboriginal Elders groups, registered native title claimants, or Aboriginal owners listed under the Aboriginal Land Rights Act 1983 register. ","The Aboriginal Cultural Heritage Advisory Committee provides advice to the Minister for Environment and Heritage on Aboriginal cultural heritage issues. The Committee advises on a range of issues including identification, assessment and protection of Aboriginal cultural heritage, heritage impact permit processes, management plans and legislative reform.",n.a. ,State,NSW,,,,,,,,,,,,,,x
N100,Aboriginal Reference Group (ARG),Network,Reference Group,Department of Communities & Justice,State Government,,,,,,"An advisory group of DCJ's Aboriginal employees provides consultations and advocacy for our work that may affect NSW Aboriginal families, clients and communities. ARG members help DCJ build working relationships with Aboriginal Elders, community groups, non-government agencies and individuals to make sure we are including Aboriginal perspectives in projects, programs and policy from the time we first start thinking about them to when they are put into action. First set up in 1985, our ARG members are elected at a regional level from around NSW. ARG promotes the inclusion of Aboriginal perspectives across: our policies and programs, the development of culturally appropriate practice and service provision, issues raised by the Minister or senior executives of DCJ, the professional development and training of our Aboriginal employees, and special projects including the creation of the DCJ website.",n.a. ,State,NSW,,,,x,,,,,,,,x,,
N101,Maranguka Working Groups,Network,Working Group,Aboriginal Legal Service (NSW/ACT) Limited,Aboriginal Community-Controlled Organisation,,,,,,"Maranguka uses quarterly working groups to bring community, government and service providers together to deliver the community-developed and community-led strategy - changing the way government, NGOs and community members service and support the community. A Cross Sector Executive meets quarterly to authorise and facilitate the work on the ground in Bourke. Maranguka and the working groups have undertaken a range of activities designed to create change within the community and the justice system. Those activities have included: Aboriginal leaders inspiring a grassroots movement for change amongst local community members, facilitating collaboration and alignment across the service system, delivering new community based programs and service hubs, and partnering with justice agencies such as the police, to evolve their procedures, behaviour and operations towards a proactive and reinvestment model of justice.",n.a. ,Regional,NSW,Western NSW,Murdi Paaki,Bourke,,,,,,,,x,x,x,
N102,Indigenous Expert Group,Network,Advisory Body,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,,,,"Provides advice and expertise to government to ensure the Supporting Healing for Families program is trauma-aware and healing-informed, and centred on Indigenous values and priorities.","Members include the Healing Foundation, SNAICC - National Voice for Our Children, the National Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation (NACCHO) and Gayaa Dhuwi (Proud Spirit) Australia.","Provides advice and expertise to government to ensure the Supporting Healing for Families program is trauma-aware and healing-informed, and centred on Indigenous values and priorities.",,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,x,,,,,,x,,x,,
N103,IPETCA Partnership Council,Network,Advisory Body,National Indigenous Australians Agency,Commonwealth Government,,,,Determines activities undertaken by the Indigenous Peoples Economic and Trade Cooperation Arrangement,,The IPETCA Partnership Council works to determine which activities are undertaken by IPETCA; and to identify opportunities for Indigenous businesses; and can propose activities that will identify and remove the barriers Indigenous people face when participating in international trade.,,National,N/A,N/A,N/A,N/A,,,,,,x,,,,,
